WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:06.740
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:09.420
It gives me great
pleasure to invite you all

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:13.320
to our continuing monthly
Inference Colloquium Series.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:17.240
These talks, followed by
discussion sessions the next day,

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:20.150
are part of our larger
intellectual project

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:22.950
on trying to understand
the nature of causation,

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:27.230
correlation, and inference
across disciplines.

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:30.650
Our series, as many of you may know,

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:34.128
has comprised talks by eminent scholars

00:00:34.128 --> 00:00:36.400
whose domains of expertise span physics,

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.000
philosophy of science, mathematics,

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.010
and including, in the coming months,

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:45.210
talks by a biologist, an
organizational behavior expert,

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.370
and a computer scientist.

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:50.360
The goals of our project
are to really understand

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:53.060
how we infer from models,

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:58.040
the power and the limits of
models as inferential engines.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:00.650
And I'm really excited
that today's speaker

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:02.990
is the philosopher of science whose work

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:06.620
is centered on physics, Dr. Elise Crull,

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:10.017
whose talk is titled,
"No Cause for Concern:

00:01:10.017 --> 00:01:11.977
"Indefinite Causal Ordering

00:01:11.977 --> 00:01:15.420
"as a Tool for
Understanding Entanglement."

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:18.650
And I'm delighted that our
conversant with Dr. Crull,

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:20.530
tomorrow's discussion session,

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:23.070
is the eminent philosopher of science,

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:25.280
Professor Yemima Ben-Menachem

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.320
from the Department of Philosophy,

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.940
the Hebrew University of Jerusalem.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:33.880
We are absolutely delighted to
have both of them participate

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:35.820
in the sequence of events.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.770
But before we start today's presentation

00:01:40.403 --> 00:01:42.060
and I introduced Dr. Crull,

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:45.070
I would like to recognize our benefactors,

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:47.580
Mr. And Mrs. Richard and Barbara Franke,

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:50.060
whose generosity in setting up

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:52.360
this unique interdisciplinary program,

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:54.720
and many other activities at Yale,

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:57.120
have greatly enriched the campus

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.490
and the university's mission.

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:01.930
Also, I need to remind those of you

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:03.500
who are assembled here today

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.420
that we are recording this event

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:09.270
and that all participants must
therefore mute their videos.

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:12.950
And as per usual, please
feel free to submit questions

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:16.080
through the chat feature
for the Q and A session

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.920
at the end of the talk.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:21.860
Now, to introduce professor Elise Crull,

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:25.050
who is a faculty member at
the Department of Philosophy

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.183
in the City University of New York.

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:31.427
After studying physics and
astronomy at Calvin University,

00:02:31.427 --> 00:02:36.427
a very robust and good intellectual
background, I might add.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:37.640
I'm joking, of course,

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:41.170
I say this as an astrophysicist myself.

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:42.940
That Professor Crull took the plunge

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:45.260
into interdisciplinary waters,

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:47.350
earning both an MA and a PhD

00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:49.430
in the history and philosophy of science

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:51.620
from the University of Notre Dame.

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:54.090
Before coming to CUNY in 2014,

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:56.020
she held postdoctoral positions

00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:57.460
at the University of Aberdeen

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:59.540
and at the Hebrew University's

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:02.290
Edelstein Center for History
and Philosophy of Science,

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:04.370
Technology and Medicine,

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:08.070
where she spent many blissful
hours huddled over documents

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:09.680
in the Einstein Archives.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:11.460
Oh gosh, I'm envious.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.890
While most of her research today concerns

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:16.010
historical and philosophical foundations

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:20.860
of quantum mechanics, Crull
is also drawn to questions

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:24.290
in sort of this hinterland
between contemporary metaphysics

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:26.950
and physics, these other really profound

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:31.570
and complex questions that
have been very, very hard

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:34.230
to even methodologically approach.

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:36.530
And Elise has made enormous headway

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:38.890
in many of these important questions.

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:40.900
In particular, she's interested in

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:43.890
the philosophical
implications of entanglement

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:48.030
and quantum decoherence,
how these phenomena modify

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:51.560
traditional views about the
structure of space time,

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:54.330
and what explanatory roles they might play

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:57.630
within various approaches
to an emerging theory

00:03:57.630 --> 00:03:59.770
of quantum gravity.

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:04.223
So without further ado,
Elise, the floor is yours.

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:08.390
- [Elise] How does everything look?

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:10.048
- [Priya] Looks great.

00:04:10.048 --> 00:04:10.980
- Fantastic.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:14.670
Well, thank you for that
generous introduction, Priya,

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:17.410
and thank you for the
invitation, also Priya and Ty,

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:20.520
to be a part of this really wonderful,

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:24.820
very deeply and genuinely
interdisciplinary colloquium

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:26.810
on inference and causation.

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:29.750
And I also wanna say
thank you to the Frankes,

00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:31.210
just for their center.

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:34.880
Such scholarship, encouraging
it among undergraduates

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:37.710
and graduates and faculty is just

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:39.560
the beating heart of the academy, I think.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:42.640
So thank you for your endeavors to create

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:44.800
these sorts of spaces for us.

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:49.800
So today, I'll be speaking
about the lack of causality

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:53.690
or indefiniteness in causality.

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:56.603
Hence, no cause for concern,

00:04:57.640 --> 00:04:59.917
I've subtitled it
"Indefinite Causal Ordering

00:04:59.917 --> 00:05:02.200
"as a Tool for
Understanding Entanglement,"

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:03.640
but I'll, in fact, be saying something

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:05.610
a little bit broader than that,

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:07.530
not just about understanding entanglement,

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:11.240
where I'll make some provisional gestures,

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:14.860
but really just thinking about
indefinite causal ordering

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:17.720
of quantum processes carefully,

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:19.720
and understanding what this new physics,

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:23.240
how it might inform various discussions

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:26.160
in quantum information,
quantum computation,

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:30.660
quantum gravity, non-relatives
as quantum mechanics,

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:33.350
and of course, in philosophy of physics.

00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:35.463
So let's dive in.

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:37.193
Okay.

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:40.670
And apologies in advance
if my cat wanders by.

00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:43.430
He just loves entanglement.

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:45.660
So I would like to begin
by setting the stage,

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:50.660
just giving you a quick
sneak peek of one small part

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:54.570
of a vast philosophical
literature on causation,

00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:58.700
and then by giving you a
sneak peek of the discussion

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:01.340
that's happening around
causality in physics,

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:03.670
just a little bit, and then I'll give you

00:06:03.670 --> 00:06:07.010
a plan for the talk, and we'll go on.

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:10.960
So here is just the brief set up.

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:15.170
Now, Matthias Fritsch has
just, in the last, I think,

00:06:15.170 --> 00:06:18.850
10 months, written a Stanford
Encyclopedia of Philosophy

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:21.310
entry on causation in physics.

00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:23.490
And I highly recommend, and
I believe there's a link

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:26.920
on the website to his article.

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:30.960
And it's just a very clear
taxonomy of the problems

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:33.550
and discussions and
debates through history.

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:34.870
But one thing he does,
which is really nice,

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:37.810
is to characterize the causation
discussion in philosophy

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:42.410
as being groups of projects
that may have overlapping,

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:45.880
but a little bit separable aims.

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:47.730
So there are metaphysical projects,

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:49.620
projects that wanna think about causation

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:54.227
and what it implies about
the way the world really is.

00:06:54.227 --> 00:06:56.060
And what is cause?

00:06:56.060 --> 00:07:00.390
What does cause mean about
spatial temporal relations?

00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:03.100
What level in the ontological hierarchy

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:06.593
should causal processes
exist in? and so on.

00:07:08.170 --> 00:07:13.170
There are also sort of
descriptive projects

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:15.980
that want to understand what is it

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:17.870
that physicists are actually doing

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:20.740
when they invoke cause or causal processes

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:24.093
or causal laws in particular theories.

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:27.120
And then there's also the functional aim.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:29.870
How does cause help us make inferences?

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:34.870
How does cause help us link
up different phenomena,

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:35.990
regimes of phenomenon?

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:37.010
And so on.

00:07:37.010 --> 00:07:42.010
So that's a quick parsing
of the philosophical space.

00:07:42.490 --> 00:07:45.020
And Yemima has written a lovely book

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:47.337
that came out a couple years ago called

00:07:47.337 --> 00:07:50.750
"Causation in Science," and
she is not primarily concerned

00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:53.600
with the metaphysical project, I believe.

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:56.200
But we'll let her say
more about that tomorrow.

00:07:56.200 --> 00:08:00.420
I will be, today, thinking,
as the first way in,

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:03.000
about metaphysics.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:06.510
And in this direction,

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:09.240
there are sort of three camps, right?

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:10.270
And here they're listed.

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:12.950
There are causal fundamentalists.

00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:14.420
I know we get sort of skeeved out

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:15.590
by the word fundamentalist,

00:08:15.590 --> 00:08:17.893
but here it just means
that causal laws are

00:08:17.893 --> 00:08:20.890
a fundamental part of the universe.

00:08:20.890 --> 00:08:23.730
So if we find some grand unifying theory,

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:28.230
whatever law sits in that
theory as sort of the basic law

00:08:28.230 --> 00:08:29.663
will be a causal law.

00:08:30.770 --> 00:08:33.127
Or it could be a thesis that says,

00:08:33.127 --> 00:08:36.470
"There exists some
universal causal principle."

00:08:36.470 --> 00:08:39.650
And this was historically
motivated for a lot of reasons,

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:42.310
because we look at the world
around us that seems that

00:08:42.310 --> 00:08:44.240
events are causally
related to one another.

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:45.280
And how do we do science?

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:48.780
We do science by
exploring such connections

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:50.810
or such relations.

00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:53.660
So these two theses come out of

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:56.310
a classical understanding the world.

00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:59.690
But in recent times, there
have been these other views,

00:08:59.690 --> 00:09:01.660
namely the causal limitivist view,

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:04.680
which was maybe made most
famous by John Wharton

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:06.660
in the last few decades.

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:11.140
And causal limitivism is just
the denial of fundamentalism.

00:09:11.140 --> 00:09:15.777
They say, "Look, there is no
fundamental causal principle."

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:20.960
Maybe cause pops up here and
there in our descriptions,

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:23.600
or it's a functional tool that we use

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:25.640
to build our theories and
make predictions and so on,

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:29.990
but it isn't a part of the
actual structure of the world.

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:33.240
So Norton wants to go even
further than just eliminating

00:09:34.150 --> 00:09:37.550
cause from the bottom level,

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:40.640
he wants to say that
cause only ever appears

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:43.060
in this sort of emergent fashion,

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:47.487
and where it appears, when
we're using that term "cause,"

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:50.540
it can always be reduced.

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:54.410
Whatever law or relationship
is being described

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:57.680
with that term can be reduced to a law

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:01.890
or a relationship that is
fundamentally non-causal.

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:04.060
In other words, what looks like causal

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:05.580
at the operational and functional level

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:07.570
disappears or reduce it to something

00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:11.120
that is ultimately non causal, right?

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:13.887
So causal limitivists,
let's eliminate cause.

00:10:13.887 --> 00:10:15.963
And it's a pretty strong position.

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:19.130
Somewhere between the last two camps

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:20.740
are the non-fundamentalists.

00:10:20.740 --> 00:10:23.340
And if you're philosophers,
you've heard about

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:26.260
the Humean Controversy or the Humeans,

00:10:26.260 --> 00:10:28.570
tend to be the non-fundamentalists.

00:10:28.570 --> 00:10:32.250
And they deny both of the theses,

00:10:32.250 --> 00:10:34.850
that there exists a
universal causal principle.

00:10:34.850 --> 00:10:38.010
And they also wanna say that
causal laws do not exist

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:39.990
as part of the fundamental
gnomic structure

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:42.970
or law-like structure of the universe.

00:10:42.970 --> 00:10:45.410
But they're gonna disagree
with the limitivist too,

00:10:45.410 --> 00:10:48.540
by saying that causal
structures or causal talk

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:53.090
can still play robust or
genuine roles in explaining,

00:10:53.090 --> 00:10:56.570
or maybe there are some
views where causality

00:10:56.570 --> 00:11:01.220
does properly emerge at some
higher level of your ontology.

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:02.700
But it isn't at the fundamental level.

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:06.803
Fundamental level is just a
Humean mosaic, if you will.

00:11:07.830 --> 00:11:11.370
So those are the three camps.

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:13.540
And I'll be looking at the physics

00:11:13.540 --> 00:11:17.510
and then sort of asking
what the physics has to say,

00:11:17.510 --> 00:11:19.030
maybe, about these three camps,

00:11:19.030 --> 00:11:21.030
if it informs our discussion in any way.

00:11:22.073 --> 00:11:24.133
All right, so what's the
discussion over in physics?

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:27.910
There are a few types of responses,

00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:30.010
crudely categorize, once again.

00:11:30.010 --> 00:11:32.350
Of course, causal assumptions are crucial

00:11:32.350 --> 00:11:35.430
for classical modeling
and in classical theories,

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:37.350
but it's been understood
for quite some time

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:41.330
that quantum mechanics allows
for non-causal situations.

00:11:41.330 --> 00:11:45.190
Whether non-causal is
equivalent to a-causal

00:11:45.190 --> 00:11:49.540
or just indefinite
causally-ordered situations

00:11:49.540 --> 00:11:52.463
will be a question we ask later on.

00:11:53.900 --> 00:11:56.670
But there are a variety
of approaches, right?

00:11:56.670 --> 00:11:58.600
There are there those theorists, usually,

00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:02.907
who say, "Look, yes, it's
possible that there is

00:12:02.907 --> 00:12:04.897
"a lack of causal structure

00:12:04.897 --> 00:12:08.033
"or there's some kind of
indefinite causal ordering, ICO,

00:12:09.677 --> 00:12:13.547
"that exists at the bottom
level in our quantum theories,

00:12:13.547 --> 00:12:16.207
"or whatever, but nevertheless,

00:12:16.207 --> 00:12:18.557
"what this should inspire
us to do is to develop

00:12:18.557 --> 00:12:21.757
"causally neutral quantum formalisms.

00:12:21.757 --> 00:12:24.617
"Or on the other hand, develop measures

00:12:24.617 --> 00:12:28.963
"for how non-causal certain
structures are," right?

00:12:30.020 --> 00:12:33.500
So maybe this is just a way
of saying we should divorce

00:12:33.500 --> 00:12:35.610
our quantum formalism from the assumption

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:38.720
of a background space time or a background

00:12:38.720 --> 00:12:40.690
causal apparatus of some sort.

00:12:40.690 --> 00:12:43.390
So I believe that Hartree Fields,

00:12:43.390 --> 00:12:46.083
causally neutral formalism
is sort of of that vein.

00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:49.390
Another formal response would be, okay,

00:12:49.390 --> 00:12:53.210
so at the bottom level we have
indefinite causal ordering,

00:12:53.210 --> 00:12:55.780
but perhaps there are
formalisms we can develop

00:12:55.780 --> 00:12:58.380
or tools we can develop
that allow us to recover

00:12:58.380 --> 00:13:00.370
causal structure.

00:13:00.370 --> 00:13:02.700
And that also is being done, for instance,

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:07.230
by Costus and Shrapnel, who developed

00:13:08.191 --> 00:13:11.253
a causal Markovian condition
for quantum systems.

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:15.990
So these people are less interested

00:13:15.990 --> 00:13:18.900
in a metaphysical question
of why we don't see cause

00:13:18.900 --> 00:13:21.780
at the bottom level, or should
we be seeing it, or whatever

00:13:21.780 --> 00:13:22.613
and are just saying, okay,

00:13:22.613 --> 00:13:25.850
given that we have the possibility

00:13:25.850 --> 00:13:29.460
of weird causal situations
from quantum mechanics,

00:13:29.460 --> 00:13:32.500
how can we adjust the framework
or the formalism up here

00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:35.440
so that we can go on
with our modeling tasks

00:13:35.440 --> 00:13:37.603
or enhance our modeling tasks?

00:13:38.830 --> 00:13:42.050
There has been an experimental response.

00:13:42.050 --> 00:13:46.277
Some of which is to say, "Okay,
this lack of causal ordering

00:13:46.277 --> 00:13:48.947
"can be a way for us
to increase efficiency

00:13:48.947 --> 00:13:51.997
"in quantum computation
or information technology

00:13:51.997 --> 00:13:56.543
"or information theory or
quantum communications," right?

00:13:57.490 --> 00:13:59.701
And this has already been done.

00:13:59.701 --> 00:14:01.450
And I'll discuss a paper where

00:14:01.450 --> 00:14:03.880
indefinite causal ordering is in fact

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:07.280
more efficiently allowed
for us to do computation

00:14:07.280 --> 00:14:09.130
of some specific problem,

00:14:09.130 --> 00:14:12.810
as opposed to a
causally-ordered superposition

00:14:12.810 --> 00:14:15.020
of a quantum surface circuit.

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:16.490
Okay.

00:14:16.490 --> 00:14:19.667
There's also a theoretical
response that says,

00:14:19.667 --> 00:14:22.353
"Well, let's look at this
lack of causal ordering

00:14:22.353 --> 00:14:24.977
"and use it to ask those deeper questions

00:14:24.977 --> 00:14:27.947
"about fundamental features
or fundamental properties

00:14:27.947 --> 00:14:30.590
"of the universe from quantum theory."

00:14:30.590 --> 00:14:35.140
So as Schrodinger said,
in his entanglement paper

00:14:35.140 --> 00:14:38.540
for the Cambridge Society in 1935,

00:14:38.540 --> 00:14:42.960
entanglement is not one, but
the characteristic property

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:45.450
that causes departure
from classical theories.

00:14:45.450 --> 00:14:48.930
So entanglement is
quantumness, in essence.

00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:53.930
And there are some theoretical
work or proposed experiments

00:14:53.960 --> 00:14:58.500
that use indefinite causal
ordering to understand

00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:02.313
or probe entanglement
in various situations.

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:04.340
So that's very cool.

00:15:04.340 --> 00:15:05.280
So it might shed...

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:07.060
We might be able to use
indefinite causal ordering

00:15:07.060 --> 00:15:10.050
to shed some light on these old questions

00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:13.160
about the underlying
properties of the world

00:15:13.160 --> 00:15:14.683
from quantum mechanics.

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:20.090
We might also use it in
our future theorizing,

00:15:20.090 --> 00:15:23.840
not just to understand
quantum mechanics per se,

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:27.660
but maybe we can look at
the lack of causal ordering

00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:32.660
as motivating certain efforts to quantize

00:15:33.260 --> 00:15:37.310
general relativity or to
quantize gravity over others.

00:15:37.310 --> 00:15:39.220
And so, I'll have more to
say about that in the future.

00:15:39.220 --> 00:15:43.440
So, a lot of responses
from the physics, as well,

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:46.900
and we'll zoom in on a few
of these that will feed,

00:15:46.900 --> 00:15:49.240
I hope, into a metaphysical-type piece.

00:15:49.240 --> 00:15:53.020
So, plan of the talk, pretty
typical philosophy of physics

00:15:53.020 --> 00:15:54.160
sort of talk.

00:15:54.160 --> 00:15:57.190
I'm gonna start by looking at
some of the physics, right?

00:15:57.190 --> 00:15:59.108
I'll ask sort of...

00:15:59.108 --> 00:16:00.230
I'll look at three things, here.

00:16:00.230 --> 00:16:02.610
First, I'll briefly discuss,

00:16:02.610 --> 00:16:04.950
since this is an
interdisciplinary audience,

00:16:04.950 --> 00:16:07.330
why would we expect
indefinite causal ordering

00:16:07.330 --> 00:16:10.460
in quantum processes in the first place?

00:16:10.460 --> 00:16:11.980
And once we've covered that ground,

00:16:11.980 --> 00:16:15.950
I'll look at a particular
experimental case,

00:16:15.950 --> 00:16:17.510
the quantum switch.

00:16:17.510 --> 00:16:22.510
And the quantum switch is
a way of measuring degrees

00:16:23.550 --> 00:16:28.550
of non-causal correlation among processes.

00:16:29.410 --> 00:16:30.730
So, it's very cool.

00:16:30.730 --> 00:16:33.430
And then I'll look at a relativistic case.

00:16:33.430 --> 00:16:37.430
So this is an experiment, well,

00:16:37.430 --> 00:16:42.050
a theoretical experiment
presented by Henderson, who's,

00:16:42.050 --> 00:16:45.190
she's at, I think,
Waterloo, and co-workers.

00:16:45.190 --> 00:16:46.777
And there, they're looking at how-

00:16:46.777 --> 00:16:49.140
- [Priya] Elise, I have a quick question.

00:16:49.140 --> 00:16:49.973
- [Elise] Sure.

00:16:49.973 --> 00:16:51.540
- [Priya] Is there a reason you are using

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:53.943
the word process rather than event?

00:16:55.960 --> 00:16:56.890
- Yes.

00:16:56.890 --> 00:16:58.277
- Okay.

00:16:58.277 --> 00:16:59.330
- And that will become
a little bit clearer

00:16:59.330 --> 00:17:02.760
when we get into the details
of the quantum switch.

00:17:02.760 --> 00:17:04.259
- Thank you.

00:17:04.259 --> 00:17:05.092
- Yeah.

00:17:05.960 --> 00:17:09.540
So Henderson and her
colleagues have been looking at

00:17:09.540 --> 00:17:13.220
quantum field theory entangling detectors

00:17:13.220 --> 00:17:14.910
that are coupled to the field,

00:17:14.910 --> 00:17:17.460
and using that to harvest entanglement.

00:17:17.460 --> 00:17:21.113
And they've found some
preliminary, very interesting,

00:17:23.580 --> 00:17:28.580
both more efficacious
ways of coupling detectors

00:17:29.910 --> 00:17:32.600
in a non-causal superposition.

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:35.400
They can harvest more entanglement

00:17:35.400 --> 00:17:38.340
with that kind of superposition
among their detectors.

00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:40.330
And also, they raised some very
interesting questions about,

00:17:40.330 --> 00:17:42.670
well, what does it really
mean to have the detectors,

00:17:42.670 --> 00:17:44.620
which are physical things, presumably,

00:17:44.620 --> 00:17:48.450
in super positions that are
physical states, presumably,

00:17:48.450 --> 00:17:51.230
that nevertheless yield
these strange sort of

00:17:51.230 --> 00:17:54.340
backwards-forwards superpositions.

00:17:54.340 --> 00:17:56.430
So look at the physics first.

00:17:56.430 --> 00:17:59.450
And then the second part will
be just sort of drawing out

00:17:59.450 --> 00:18:02.240
some of the consequences of this physics.

00:18:02.240 --> 00:18:07.090
And this is the part where I'm
doing totally new stuff here

00:18:07.090 --> 00:18:07.923
with you all, now.

00:18:07.923 --> 00:18:09.480
And so I'd love to have lots of discussion

00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:10.680
with you all about it.

00:18:10.680 --> 00:18:14.660
So again, I'll sort of
look at the quantum switch

00:18:14.660 --> 00:18:16.900
and what it might imply or not imply

00:18:16.900 --> 00:18:19.690
about causation in quantum physics.

00:18:19.690 --> 00:18:22.080
And then I'll look at
the case of entanglement,

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:26.200
harvesting, and QFT, and sort of have

00:18:26.200 --> 00:18:28.543
a think about what that might mean.

00:18:28.543 --> 00:18:29.850
And then part three is very quick.

00:18:29.850 --> 00:18:34.850
It's just where I'm gonna
make some talk-sized theses,

00:18:35.710 --> 00:18:40.336
the specific talk-sized
thesis about causality

00:18:40.336 --> 00:18:43.080
and quantum theories, what can we say,

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:47.160
given ICO as an active area of theoretical

00:18:47.160 --> 00:18:49.690
and experimental research right now?

00:18:49.690 --> 00:18:52.340
What can we conclude about
causality in quantum?

00:18:52.340 --> 00:18:56.080
But also, maybe, in physics
more generally, maybe.

00:18:56.080 --> 00:18:59.130
And then a general thesis,
which isn't about physics alone,

00:18:59.130 --> 00:19:01.440
but as a back to that
question I started with

00:19:01.440 --> 00:19:04.635
about the metaphysics of causation itself.

00:19:04.635 --> 00:19:08.090
Has any of the physics that I go through,

00:19:08.090 --> 00:19:12.380
has it modified or made
easier or more difficult

00:19:12.380 --> 00:19:16.430
certain aspects or defenses
for those three main approaches

00:19:16.430 --> 00:19:17.830
to the metaphysics of causation,

00:19:17.830 --> 00:19:21.133
the Humean non-fundamentalist account,

00:19:21.133 --> 00:19:25.210
the non-Humean sort of
fundamentalist account,

00:19:25.210 --> 00:19:26.653
and then the elimitavists?

00:19:27.590 --> 00:19:29.313
Okay, here we go.

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:35.913
So, the physics.

00:19:37.540 --> 00:19:41.460
Why do we expect
indefinite causal ordering

00:19:41.460 --> 00:19:44.343
in quantum processes in the first place?

00:19:45.490 --> 00:19:49.970
Well, in the literature, and I think

00:19:49.970 --> 00:19:51.470
there's some care that needs to be taken

00:19:51.470 --> 00:19:53.280
from philosophers here, too.

00:19:53.280 --> 00:19:55.560
because in the literature
it's very quickly said

00:19:55.560 --> 00:19:57.427
by the physicist that,
"Well, there are sort of

00:19:57.427 --> 00:20:00.107
"these two reasons that
come from the formalism

00:20:00.107 --> 00:20:02.597
"of quantum mechanics
that lead us to expect

00:20:02.597 --> 00:20:05.597
"uncertainty with respect to everything,

00:20:05.597 --> 00:20:07.907
"including causal properties,

00:20:07.907 --> 00:20:11.197
"but maybe also the experimental violation

00:20:11.197 --> 00:20:13.257
"of Bell inequalities
tells us also something

00:20:13.257 --> 00:20:14.567
"about causality."

00:20:15.780 --> 00:20:19.670
But as I note, two thirds of
the way down the page, there,

00:20:19.670 --> 00:20:23.040
there may be different
notions of non-classical

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:26.750
causal structure being implied
by these different things.

00:20:26.750 --> 00:20:28.890
So keep that in mind.

00:20:28.890 --> 00:20:31.150
So here's the theory reason number one.

00:20:31.150 --> 00:20:33.567
It's just the Superposition
Principle, right?

00:20:35.180 --> 00:20:37.300
We do quantum mechanics in Hilbert space.

00:20:37.300 --> 00:20:39.490
And one of the properties
of Hilbert space is

00:20:39.490 --> 00:20:41.090
the superposition principle, namely,

00:20:41.090 --> 00:20:44.450
if side one is a
solution, a possible state

00:20:44.450 --> 00:20:49.450
for some system or a process
that systems can undergo,

00:20:50.040 --> 00:20:52.170
and side two is, likewise, a solution,

00:20:52.170 --> 00:20:56.780
an event, a process, then any
linear combination of them

00:20:56.780 --> 00:20:57.700
is also a solution.

00:20:57.700 --> 00:20:59.540
So you've probably heard this described

00:20:59.540 --> 00:21:01.716
in the double-slit experiment.

00:21:01.716 --> 00:21:05.000
If passage of an electron
or a photon through one slit

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:07.850
is a possible solution, it is,

00:21:07.850 --> 00:21:10.420
and passage through the other
slit is a possible solution,

00:21:10.420 --> 00:21:14.290
it is, then a superposition
of passage through slit one

00:21:14.290 --> 00:21:17.750
and slit two is also a possible solution,

00:21:17.750 --> 00:21:20.480
a possible state that
the electron could have,

00:21:20.480 --> 00:21:22.220
or the photon, right?

00:21:22.220 --> 00:21:25.670
So what this means for
causal processes is that

00:21:25.670 --> 00:21:30.070
it's possible that there's a state,

00:21:30.070 --> 00:21:32.760
but state here is broader
than state before,

00:21:32.760 --> 00:21:36.780
because it characterizes
not an event, but a process,

00:21:36.780 --> 00:21:40.140
resulting from two
ordered simple operations.

00:21:40.140 --> 00:21:44.470
So you could have a process
where Operation A happens first,

00:21:44.470 --> 00:21:46.550
then Operation B happens.

00:21:46.550 --> 00:21:48.940
And that process is in a superposition

00:21:48.940 --> 00:21:51.563
with the process where B
happens first and then A.

00:21:52.550 --> 00:21:56.770
Now, notice I've left
it vague in this setup,

00:21:56.770 --> 00:21:59.690
whether there's a causal
relationship between A and B

00:21:59.690 --> 00:22:03.390
or whether it's just a
temporal relationship.

00:22:03.390 --> 00:22:05.697
And that will make a
difference when we get to

00:22:05.697 --> 00:22:09.280
the entanglement harvesting
quantum field theory work

00:22:09.280 --> 00:22:10.600
by Henderson and colleagues.

00:22:10.600 --> 00:22:15.600
Okay, so prima facie, the
superposition principle

00:22:15.820 --> 00:22:19.390
would suggest that superpositions
of processes are possible

00:22:19.390 --> 00:22:22.340
in the same way that superpositions
of states are possible.

00:22:24.640 --> 00:22:27.420
Theoretical reason number two
is the uncertainty principle,

00:22:27.420 --> 00:22:29.363
Heisenberg's uncertainty principle,

00:22:30.880 --> 00:22:35.880
that there's a bound on
the precision with which

00:22:38.270 --> 00:22:41.320
canonical variables,
commuting variables, rather,

00:22:41.320 --> 00:22:43.863
can be simultaneously measured.

00:22:46.650 --> 00:22:48.320
The question that many of the physicists

00:22:48.320 --> 00:22:50.640
begin their paper with,
who are doing ICO is,

00:22:50.640 --> 00:22:52.790
well, if there's uncertainty
regarding property,

00:22:52.790 --> 00:22:55.790
classical properties like
position and momentum

00:22:55.790 --> 00:22:58.430
and velocity, then it stands
to reason that causality,

00:22:58.430 --> 00:23:00.000
which is sort of a meta relation,

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:04.890
a relation that comes out of
the relative space and time

00:23:04.890 --> 00:23:08.660
and momentum and velocity
of the systems in question,

00:23:08.660 --> 00:23:11.360
then surely there's uncertainty
at that level as well.

00:23:12.800 --> 00:23:15.180
And here's my little nota bene,

00:23:15.180 --> 00:23:18.040
maybe there's a different kind
of uncertainty that arises

00:23:18.040 --> 00:23:20.423
through to causal superpositions,

00:23:21.330 --> 00:23:23.160
a superposition of one causal ordering

00:23:23.160 --> 00:23:24.830
with a superposition of
another causal ordering

00:23:24.830 --> 00:23:28.100
that seems to be a
different state of affairs

00:23:28.100 --> 00:23:30.260
than just saying that
there's indefiniteness

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:34.923
or that there's uncertainty
in some causal process.

00:23:36.230 --> 00:23:38.130
So, right.

00:23:38.130 --> 00:23:39.860
Hi cat, are you weighing in?

00:23:39.860 --> 00:23:40.960
Good.

00:23:40.960 --> 00:23:43.560
The experimental reason for expecting

00:23:43.560 --> 00:23:46.440
indefinite causal ordering
in quantum processes

00:23:46.440 --> 00:23:50.350
is just ye old Bell's inequalities, right?

00:23:50.350 --> 00:23:54.470
If you have an EPR pair
of entangled particles

00:23:54.470 --> 00:23:56.600
and you send them shooting off
in some different directions,

00:23:56.600 --> 00:23:58.933
you have spatially separated detectors.

00:24:00.190 --> 00:24:03.420
And you measure sort of the
correlation between, I dunno,

00:24:03.420 --> 00:24:05.580
polarization of those photons.

00:24:05.580 --> 00:24:07.200
And you look at the statistics,

00:24:07.200 --> 00:24:09.433
the amount of times
they agree or disagree.

00:24:10.370 --> 00:24:14.530
Classical probability
gives you some upper bound

00:24:14.530 --> 00:24:16.700
of how often you would
expect them to agree

00:24:16.700 --> 00:24:19.930
when you set the detectors to
different angles, and so on.

00:24:19.930 --> 00:24:23.937
Bell's inequality says, "Look,
if there's a correlation

00:24:23.937 --> 00:24:28.047
"greater than this amount,
then it just can't be described

00:24:28.047 --> 00:24:30.780
"by classical processes," right?

00:24:30.780 --> 00:24:34.020
So there's already causal
notions packed into

00:24:34.020 --> 00:24:35.820
the Bell inequality.

00:24:35.820 --> 00:24:38.590
And as many of you are well aware,

00:24:38.590 --> 00:24:43.590
interpreting what Bell
tests mean about locality

00:24:44.390 --> 00:24:47.820
or separability or
entanglement or causation

00:24:47.820 --> 00:24:50.650
has not only spawned new interpretations

00:24:50.650 --> 00:24:54.450
of quantum mechanics, but
spawned a robust literature.

00:24:54.450 --> 00:24:57.080
So it's a complex question.

00:24:57.080 --> 00:25:02.080
But the very rough idea that
Bell's Theorem provides about,

00:25:02.640 --> 00:25:06.660
on what a local theory
should be able to describe

00:25:06.660 --> 00:25:11.660
versus what a non-local
theory would give rise to,

00:25:11.840 --> 00:25:15.190
is sort of considered
by many ICO physicists

00:25:15.190 --> 00:25:18.050
to be analogous to what
they're trying to do.

00:25:18.050 --> 00:25:21.460
They wanna find bounds that tell them

00:25:21.460 --> 00:25:24.280
whether processes are existing in causal

00:25:24.280 --> 00:25:26.123
or non-causal orders.

00:25:28.100 --> 00:25:28.933
Okay.

00:25:33.170 --> 00:25:37.150
So, a little bit of the background, here,

00:25:37.150 --> 00:25:40.470
the basic theory of a quantum switch.

00:25:40.470 --> 00:25:42.783
And so just, if you don't remember,

00:25:42.783 --> 00:25:46.020
quantum cubit is just a
two-state quantum system,

00:25:46.020 --> 00:25:47.950
so like a spin half particle or something.

00:25:47.950 --> 00:25:50.943
So imagine we got two two-qubit states,

00:25:51.990 --> 00:25:55.910
and the two-qubit composite
state capital psi,

00:25:55.910 --> 00:25:57.900
where you've got a control Qubit C

00:25:57.900 --> 00:26:00.650
and a target Qubit Phi T,

00:26:00.650 --> 00:26:02.970
and they live in a joint Hilbert space,

00:26:02.970 --> 00:26:05.650
all that's pretty normal
quantum mechanics.

00:26:05.650 --> 00:26:09.770
But now we can set up a scenario

00:26:09.770 --> 00:26:11.880
where we condition two
different operations,

00:26:11.880 --> 00:26:15.950
Operation A, which could just
be a measurement in a lab,

00:26:15.950 --> 00:26:20.730
and Operation B, and apply
them to our target state.

00:26:20.730 --> 00:26:23.620
So depending on what value
the control state has,

00:26:23.620 --> 00:26:27.860
the target state will be
operated on in a different order.

00:26:27.860 --> 00:26:30.633
So we might categorize
this down here as bottom,

00:26:31.682 --> 00:26:36.682
that if the control
state is at value zero,

00:26:40.275 --> 00:26:43.260
or in state zero, or spin up, if you will,

00:26:43.260 --> 00:26:46.140
then we're gonna perform
the Operation A first,

00:26:46.140 --> 00:26:49.280
then B, on our target qubit.

00:26:49.280 --> 00:26:52.360
But if the control qubit
is in, I don't know,

00:26:52.360 --> 00:26:55.130
the other states, so spin down, maybe,

00:26:55.130 --> 00:26:56.420
then we're gonna perform the operations

00:26:56.420 --> 00:26:59.063
in the reverse order on our target qubit.

00:27:02.890 --> 00:27:07.100
So we just went through the
superposition principle.

00:27:07.100 --> 00:27:08.190
Should be pretty obvious,

00:27:08.190 --> 00:27:11.940
that if the control qubit is
able to be in zero or one,

00:27:11.940 --> 00:27:13.810
then it follows that it's possible

00:27:13.810 --> 00:27:17.480
that it's in a superposition
of zero and one, right?

00:27:17.480 --> 00:27:18.660
Maybe a maximally...

00:27:19.844 --> 00:27:21.940
A nice superposition right here.

00:27:21.940 --> 00:27:26.180
And what results from this is
a superposition of operations.

00:27:26.180 --> 00:27:29.480
So you have the process, first A then B,

00:27:29.480 --> 00:27:32.423
in a superposition with the
process, first B than A.

00:27:33.530 --> 00:27:37.350
And note that this
superposition of processes

00:27:39.210 --> 00:27:42.950
is itself a superposition,
but also is itself entangled

00:27:42.950 --> 00:27:46.550
with the control qubit state.

00:27:46.550 --> 00:27:48.253
So it's non-separable already.

00:27:49.090 --> 00:27:50.890
So this is a really
interesting little image,

00:27:50.890 --> 00:27:53.500
but I think it's helpful,
is from Rubino et al.

00:27:53.500 --> 00:27:55.470
And Rubino and his
colleagues were the first,

00:27:55.470 --> 00:27:59.770
as far as I know, to
experimentally directly verify

00:27:59.770 --> 00:28:02.823
the existence of
indefinite causal ordering.

00:28:04.190 --> 00:28:06.120
And so here, we've got our process matrix

00:28:06.120 --> 00:28:07.300
at the bottom, W, who...

00:28:07.300 --> 00:28:08.133
I don't have a point...

00:28:08.133 --> 00:28:09.583
Can you see my pointer?

00:28:13.110 --> 00:28:15.173
Well, don't-
- I think so, Elise.

00:28:16.312 --> 00:28:17.180
- [Elise] You can?

00:28:17.180 --> 00:28:18.013
- [Priya] Yes.

00:28:18.013 --> 00:28:18.846
- All right.

00:28:18.846 --> 00:28:21.370
So at the bottom of each
of these three panes,

00:28:21.370 --> 00:28:23.573
which are three different scenarios,

00:28:24.690 --> 00:28:29.300
we have the process matrix, W,

00:28:29.300 --> 00:28:33.660
which tells us what the
path was, what the order was

00:28:33.660 --> 00:28:35.110
for the target qubit.

00:28:35.110 --> 00:28:37.730
So here's where we're
saying the control qubit is

00:28:37.730 --> 00:28:40.910
in state zero, or spin up, if you wish.

00:28:40.910 --> 00:28:43.660
In which case the target qubit is...

00:28:43.660 --> 00:28:45.410
We'll say that this is Alice.

00:28:45.410 --> 00:28:49.360
Alice is in her
independent lab in England,

00:28:49.360 --> 00:28:51.270
and she does a measurement
on target qubit,

00:28:51.270 --> 00:28:53.980
and then sends it over to Bob.

00:28:53.980 --> 00:28:57.600
And Bob is in Istanbul, and
Bob does the measurement,

00:28:57.600 --> 00:29:00.810
and he performs process P on it.

00:29:00.810 --> 00:29:02.960
And then we get an output, right?

00:29:02.960 --> 00:29:04.540
It could go the other direction, as well,

00:29:04.540 --> 00:29:08.630
where first Bob gets the target
qubit, and then Alice does.

00:29:08.630 --> 00:29:11.233
And then this final state
is the one of interest

00:29:11.233 --> 00:29:13.790
for quantum switch, which is
when we have the control qubit

00:29:13.790 --> 00:29:16.730
in a superposition of those processes.

00:29:16.730 --> 00:29:20.653
In which case, the target
qubit performs a superposition

00:29:23.180 --> 00:29:27.543
of both of the prior processes, right?

00:29:28.400 --> 00:29:33.400
So it's unclear what
causal ordering is going on

00:29:35.170 --> 00:29:37.050
between Alice and Bob.

00:29:37.050 --> 00:29:41.190
It's not predetermined by the setup.

00:29:41.190 --> 00:29:44.040
Well, I'll say that a little
more about that in a moment.

00:29:46.050 --> 00:29:47.850
So another way to characterize this,

00:29:49.300 --> 00:29:51.630
in a quantum switch, we've got W.

00:29:51.630 --> 00:29:53.640
The particular ordering is it first does

00:29:53.640 --> 00:29:55.010
this process A, here.

00:29:55.010 --> 00:29:56.040
It first...

00:29:56.040 --> 00:29:58.490
Sorry, it goes the other direction.

00:29:58.490 --> 00:30:01.390
It first puts the input
state through a superposition

00:30:01.390 --> 00:30:04.530
of A and B, and that
gives us some output one,

00:30:04.530 --> 00:30:05.920
in parentheses.

00:30:05.920 --> 00:30:09.540
Then it sends the input through A then B,

00:30:09.540 --> 00:30:11.270
which results in output two.

00:30:11.270 --> 00:30:13.610
And then through B then A,
resulting in output three,

00:30:13.610 --> 00:30:16.980
and then it coherently
recombines all of those outputs.

00:30:16.980 --> 00:30:21.040
And that measurement is the
measurement we're interested in.

00:30:21.040 --> 00:30:26.040
It will tell us what degree,
if any, of non-causal ordering

00:30:29.890 --> 00:30:33.800
was evident between the
processes: A to B, then B to A.

00:30:36.270 --> 00:30:40.820
So in order to make this measurement

00:30:40.820 --> 00:30:45.550
or quantify the degree of entanglement

00:30:45.550 --> 00:30:49.340
or non-separability
between these causal states

00:30:49.340 --> 00:30:50.973
or causal processes, forgive me,

00:30:52.570 --> 00:30:56.030
the physicists build a causal witness,

00:30:56.030 --> 00:30:59.300
which is an operator that's
supposed to be analogous

00:30:59.300 --> 00:31:01.000
to an entanglement witness.

00:31:01.000 --> 00:31:03.400
So just in case you don't
know, an entanglement witness

00:31:03.400 --> 00:31:06.650
is just a Hermitian operator
that, when it acts on

00:31:06.650 --> 00:31:09.940
some composite system, will
give you a density matrix.

00:31:09.940 --> 00:31:14.490
When you carry out the trace
of that composite system

00:31:14.490 --> 00:31:15.600
with the operator acting on it,

00:31:15.600 --> 00:31:17.040
if the trace is less than zero,

00:31:17.040 --> 00:31:21.290
you can conclude that the
subsystems are entangled,

00:31:21.290 --> 00:31:22.980
and to what degree.

00:31:22.980 --> 00:31:25.600
And if the trace is greater
than or equal to zero,

00:31:25.600 --> 00:31:30.170
then you conclude that the
composite system was separable.

00:31:30.170 --> 00:31:34.220
So analogously, but instead
of using a density matrix,

00:31:34.220 --> 00:31:36.830
here we wanna use the process matrix W.

00:31:36.830 --> 00:31:39.770
And so here's the difference
between a density matrix

00:31:39.770 --> 00:31:44.770
and states with a process
matrix W and processes.

00:31:48.280 --> 00:31:50.190
So a causal witness, then, is just

00:31:52.545 --> 00:31:55.610
a specially-defined
Hermitian operator that acts

00:31:55.610 --> 00:32:00.040
on the process matrix
W, and which describes

00:32:00.040 --> 00:32:02.040
the state of the operations.

00:32:02.040 --> 00:32:04.320
And again, it yields a
trace of less than zero

00:32:04.320 --> 00:32:09.320
if the processes it's witnessing
are causally separable.

00:32:10.120 --> 00:32:14.190
So we can say that A
then B happened first,

00:32:14.190 --> 00:32:16.393
then B then A happened, or vice versa.

00:32:17.550 --> 00:32:20.680
But the measure is greater
than or equal to zero

00:32:20.680 --> 00:32:21.580
if they're entangled.

00:32:21.580 --> 00:32:24.380
And I believe it is flipped like that,

00:32:24.380 --> 00:32:27.490
so it's less than zero
if they're separable

00:32:27.490 --> 00:32:31.000
and greater than or equal to
zero if they're entangled.

00:32:31.000 --> 00:32:34.240
And so, of course, much like
with the Bell inequalities,

00:32:34.240 --> 00:32:37.170
you can come up with
sort of this upper bound,

00:32:37.170 --> 00:32:40.120
or with these limitations
for what you expect,

00:32:40.120 --> 00:32:43.123
for degrees of entanglement
between these causal processes.

00:32:44.440 --> 00:32:47.950
And experimental agreement is high.

00:32:47.950 --> 00:32:50.113
So just to back up a little bit,

00:32:51.930 --> 00:32:56.480
and maybe put this in
slightly more layman's terms,

00:32:56.480 --> 00:32:59.950
I suppose, Rushkoff, and
his colleagues in 2012

00:32:59.950 --> 00:33:02.809
sort of gave a theoretical picture of what

00:33:02.809 --> 00:33:05.830
this process matrix might be like.

00:33:05.830 --> 00:33:08.540
And they assume that there's this game

00:33:08.540 --> 00:33:09.740
between Alice and Bob.

00:33:09.740 --> 00:33:13.750
I mean, Bob has some
strategy where he's gonna get

00:33:13.750 --> 00:33:16.610
his system, he's gonna
receive it from Alice.

00:33:16.610 --> 00:33:19.300
And if what he receives from
Alice is a particular state,

00:33:19.300 --> 00:33:22.150
then he's gonna do a
particular kind of measurement.

00:33:22.150 --> 00:33:23.580
And if it's a different kind of state,

00:33:23.580 --> 00:33:25.270
then he'll do a different
kind of measurement.

00:33:25.270 --> 00:33:30.270
So his output is conditional
analysis, and so on.

00:33:30.310 --> 00:33:33.740
So there's a causal connection
between what Bob does,

00:33:33.740 --> 00:33:35.480
the measurement he does,

00:33:35.480 --> 00:33:37.933
and what Alice is doing over in her lab.

00:33:38.950 --> 00:33:42.370
And so, Rushkoff and his
colleagues calculate that

00:33:42.370 --> 00:33:45.270
if you assume that there's a causal order,

00:33:45.270 --> 00:33:48.100
that it's very clear that

00:33:51.580 --> 00:33:56.580
Alice sends her states to
Bob, he does his measurements,

00:33:57.160 --> 00:34:01.480
and he's successful,
conditional on the input

00:34:01.480 --> 00:34:05.720
of Alice's lab, they say
that the success will be

00:34:05.720 --> 00:34:07.230
less than three fourths of the time

00:34:07.230 --> 00:34:09.370
he's gonna be able to implement a strategy

00:34:09.370 --> 00:34:10.620
that is successful, okay?

00:34:11.590 --> 00:34:14.150
However, if they set up this...

00:34:14.150 --> 00:34:18.510
Again, it's like a superposition
of the causal ordering

00:34:18.510 --> 00:34:20.770
in the quantum switch, right?

00:34:20.770 --> 00:34:23.570
If Alice and Bob, the
order that they receive

00:34:23.570 --> 00:34:27.020
each other's information or states

00:34:27.020 --> 00:34:28.770
is not determined beforehand,

00:34:28.770 --> 00:34:30.660
so it's not that Alice
sends results to Bob

00:34:30.660 --> 00:34:33.260
and Bob makes a conditional decision,

00:34:33.260 --> 00:34:35.620
if it's indeterminate, then, in fact,

00:34:35.620 --> 00:34:39.010
Bob's rate of success at
adjusting his measurement

00:34:39.010 --> 00:34:43.053
based on what he received as
input from Alice is higher.

00:34:46.760 --> 00:34:50.040
This is exciting because, at
least for Rushkoff and others,

00:34:50.040 --> 00:34:53.130
it shows that, perhaps, if you do have

00:34:53.130 --> 00:34:58.130
a indefinite causal ordering
between these two operations,

00:34:58.370 --> 00:35:01.140
you can get a higher efficiency.

00:35:01.140 --> 00:35:03.440
You can get a success rate
for certain computations

00:35:03.440 --> 00:35:05.920
that's higher than if you're dealing with

00:35:05.920 --> 00:35:10.610
causally-ordered sets of operations.

00:35:10.610 --> 00:35:12.850
And that was, in fact, what was proven

00:35:12.850 --> 00:35:15.770
in Procopio et al, 2013.

00:35:15.770 --> 00:35:19.440
So they indirectly verified
the indefinite causal ordering

00:35:19.440 --> 00:35:20.850
of a quantum switch.

00:35:20.850 --> 00:35:23.380
So they didn't use a causal witness.

00:35:23.380 --> 00:35:25.810
So they didn't get an exact
measure on the entanglement.

00:35:25.810 --> 00:35:27.160
But what they did was they showed that

00:35:27.160 --> 00:35:31.660
they could get higher
efficiency on some computation

00:35:31.660 --> 00:35:34.740
when using a quantum circuit that was in

00:35:34.740 --> 00:35:38.210
a causal superposition,
as opposed to a circuit

00:35:38.210 --> 00:35:43.210
that was causally
ordered in its processes.

00:35:43.940 --> 00:35:47.997
So the teams listed below said,

00:35:47.997 --> 00:35:49.177
"Okay, well, we wanna do one better.

00:35:49.177 --> 00:35:53.747
"We want to implement
this causal witness schema

00:35:53.747 --> 00:35:56.537
"and see if we can directly verify

00:35:56.537 --> 00:35:58.560
"indefinite causal ordering."

00:35:58.560 --> 00:36:01.110
Because in a sense, what
Procopia et al had done

00:36:01.110 --> 00:36:04.570
is just to show that the
superposition was more effective

00:36:04.570 --> 00:36:07.313
than sort of separable causal ordering.

00:36:08.310 --> 00:36:11.570
But Rubin et al and Boswami et al

00:36:12.651 --> 00:36:13.600
wanted to get quantitative about it.

00:36:13.600 --> 00:36:16.420
So they developed the causal witness.

00:36:16.420 --> 00:36:18.890
And they did in fact measure values

00:36:18.890 --> 00:36:20.150
that were in close agreement with

00:36:20.150 --> 00:36:22.930
the theoretical expectation for

00:36:22.930 --> 00:36:26.220
non-separable causal processes.

00:36:26.220 --> 00:36:30.690
So upshot here, these direct verifications

00:36:30.690 --> 00:36:34.960
of indefinite causal ordering are showing

00:36:34.960 --> 00:36:39.010
our measurements of
entangled causal processes,

00:36:39.010 --> 00:36:42.790
of processes where order
A and B is entangled

00:36:42.790 --> 00:36:44.203
with B and A, and so on.

00:36:46.420 --> 00:36:49.673
Pretty bizarre. (laughs)

00:36:52.140 --> 00:36:55.530
I would like to talk briefly about

00:36:55.530 --> 00:36:58.600
what might be happening
in quantum field theory.

00:36:58.600 --> 00:37:01.280
And as far as I'm aware,
a lot of this work

00:37:01.280 --> 00:37:04.810
stems from something Brookner
said in a 2014 paper,

00:37:04.810 --> 00:37:08.110
which is sort of a review of the field

00:37:08.110 --> 00:37:09.847
physics and causality right now.

00:37:09.847 --> 00:37:12.510
And so, he made this
interesting suggestion

00:37:12.510 --> 00:37:14.937
after describing the
quantum switch, saying,

00:37:14.937 --> 00:37:17.887
"Well, look, we could probably
do something analogous

00:37:17.887 --> 00:37:20.157
"using a massive object
that's in a superposition

00:37:20.157 --> 00:37:22.017
"of positions."

00:37:23.470 --> 00:37:25.700
Because if they're spatially separated,

00:37:25.700 --> 00:37:28.980
the component states have
some spatial separation

00:37:28.980 --> 00:37:30.393
that they're entangled over,

00:37:31.350 --> 00:37:32.730
that means that the gravitational field

00:37:32.730 --> 00:37:34.160
that that massive object produces

00:37:34.160 --> 00:37:36.200
will also be in a superposition of states.

00:37:36.200 --> 00:37:38.450
Ergo, the space-time geometry itself

00:37:38.450 --> 00:37:40.573
will be in a superposition.

00:37:40.573 --> 00:37:42.557
And so, I have this quote
from him here, that,

00:37:42.557 --> 00:37:44.657
"The causal succession of gate operations

00:37:44.657 --> 00:37:47.103
"in a quantum circuit," or quantum switch,

00:37:47.997 --> 00:37:51.457
"is the analog of events
separated by a time-like interval.

00:37:51.457 --> 00:37:52.867
"This analogy can be pushed further.

00:37:52.867 --> 00:37:55.404
"The networks in which
geometry of the wires

00:37:55.404 --> 00:37:56.671
"between the gates is entangled with

00:37:56.671 --> 00:37:58.000
"the state of the control qubit,"

00:37:58.000 --> 00:38:00.127
so again, the quantum switch situation,

00:38:00.127 --> 00:38:01.717
"Can be thought of as a toy model

00:38:01.717 --> 00:38:04.590
"for the quantum gravity situation,"

00:38:04.590 --> 00:38:06.640
that he introduced before.

00:38:06.640 --> 00:38:08.967
Namely, "If you put a massive object

00:38:08.967 --> 00:38:10.327
"in a spatial superposition,

00:38:10.327 --> 00:38:11.787
"then the metric that's produced,

00:38:11.787 --> 00:38:14.377
"hence spatiotemporal
distances between the events

00:38:14.377 --> 00:38:17.097
"in the gravitational field get entangled

00:38:17.097 --> 00:38:19.030
"with the object's position."

00:38:19.030 --> 00:38:22.250
So this suggests new ways
to understand entanglement

00:38:23.610 --> 00:38:25.910
at different locations in a field,

00:38:25.910 --> 00:38:29.520
and also to possibly motivate or constrain

00:38:29.520 --> 00:38:32.440
the ways we quantize general relativity.

00:38:32.440 --> 00:38:36.850
So to that end, very recently,

00:38:36.850 --> 00:38:39.917
here's the Henderson et al
paper that I mentioned earlier,

00:38:39.917 --> 00:38:43.620
"Quantum Temporal
Superposition: The Case of QFT."

00:38:43.620 --> 00:38:46.190
I'm gonna be really brief on this

00:38:46.190 --> 00:38:48.090
because I wanna get to the philosophy.

00:38:50.030 --> 00:38:52.210
Quantum field theories
describe correlations

00:38:52.210 --> 00:38:53.600
between space-time points.

00:38:53.600 --> 00:38:54.720
That's what they do.

00:38:54.720 --> 00:38:57.360
So if we put a really tiny little,

00:38:57.360 --> 00:39:00.420
like a quantum system
that's a particle detector

00:39:00.420 --> 00:39:03.280
in the field and we
couple it to the field,

00:39:03.280 --> 00:39:05.030
then it can perform
measurements on the field

00:39:05.030 --> 00:39:06.560
and can tell us about local values.

00:39:06.560 --> 00:39:09.500
It can tell us about
correlations, and so on.

00:39:09.500 --> 00:39:13.130
So these physicists asked the question,

00:39:13.130 --> 00:39:16.323
and you notice Brookner is
among the colleagues here.

00:39:17.210 --> 00:39:20.500
So maybe he made that 2014 suggestion

00:39:20.500 --> 00:39:22.680
with malice aforethought.

00:39:22.680 --> 00:39:25.960
But question, what if we
put two particle detectors

00:39:25.960 --> 00:39:28.810
out in the field that are
in a quantum superposition

00:39:28.810 --> 00:39:33.810
and then use that composite
or superposed detectors,

00:39:34.950 --> 00:39:38.190
use it to probe field correlations?

00:39:38.190 --> 00:39:40.200
Another way to say that is, in particular,

00:39:40.200 --> 00:39:44.270
to harvest entanglement, to
measure degrees of correlation,

00:39:44.270 --> 00:39:47.010
quantum correlation, in the field.

00:39:47.010 --> 00:39:49.500
Will we, in analogy with a quantum switch,

00:39:49.500 --> 00:39:52.670
find higher efficacy
when the detectors are

00:39:52.670 --> 00:39:53.890
in superposed states?

00:39:53.890 --> 00:39:56.390
Will they be able to measure
a higher degree of...

00:39:57.520 --> 00:40:00.320
More effectively measure
entanglement, rather?

00:40:00.320 --> 00:40:02.570
And it looks like that
is, in fact, the case.

00:40:04.210 --> 00:40:07.160
So they looked at two
different situations here.

00:40:07.160 --> 00:40:08.230
So they took...

00:40:09.536 --> 00:40:10.820
So on the left-hand side, here,

00:40:10.820 --> 00:40:15.520
they have the two different detectors

00:40:15.520 --> 00:40:20.420
that are in a cause-effect superposition.

00:40:20.420 --> 00:40:22.040
So it's an explicit superposition

00:40:22.040 --> 00:40:25.830
where A will cause some
later state in detector B,

00:40:25.830 --> 00:40:28.430
and B causes A, right?

00:40:28.430 --> 00:40:31.450
Whereas on the right side,
it's just the past future.

00:40:31.450 --> 00:40:35.660
So it's just a temporal
ordering, A before B,

00:40:35.660 --> 00:40:38.590
that is the time that the
detector takes a measurement.

00:40:38.590 --> 00:40:40.763
A before B and then B before A.

00:40:42.698 --> 00:40:44.220
They found some weird...

00:40:45.530 --> 00:40:48.560
First of all, that they could
propose this theoretically

00:40:48.560 --> 00:40:53.560
is quite something, because
there are no-go theorems

00:40:54.630 --> 00:40:57.260
about how the signaling among
the superimposed detectors

00:40:57.260 --> 00:40:58.600
can work, and so on.

00:40:58.600 --> 00:41:01.040
It was quite a feat.

00:41:01.040 --> 00:41:02.970
But it's interesting
because they found out that

00:41:02.970 --> 00:41:05.860
in each, in the left-hand case
and in the right-hand case

00:41:05.860 --> 00:41:10.860
in this image, you have different branches

00:41:10.940 --> 00:41:12.370
of the superposition.

00:41:12.370 --> 00:41:15.030
And while you can transform one branch,

00:41:15.030 --> 00:41:17.900
either from a cause-effect
superposition to...

00:41:17.900 --> 00:41:21.863
Sorry, from a cause-effect
situation to a past-future,

00:41:23.010 --> 00:41:26.600
you cannot ever do that so
that both branches agree

00:41:26.600 --> 00:41:30.030
in cause-effect, whether
they're cause-effect relations

00:41:30.030 --> 00:41:32.743
or past-future relations.

00:41:35.032 --> 00:41:37.720
So there's sort of that interesting way

00:41:37.720 --> 00:41:40.860
that it looks like they're able to tell,

00:41:40.860 --> 00:41:43.700
by coupling these detectors in
the right way with the field,

00:41:43.700 --> 00:41:46.280
and they're being in
superposition with one another,

00:41:46.280 --> 00:41:49.740
they can differentiate
between superpositions

00:41:49.740 --> 00:41:52.750
that are just A before B and B before A,

00:41:52.750 --> 00:41:57.750
versus legitimate
superpositions of A causes B,

00:41:58.590 --> 00:42:00.890
with B causes A.

00:42:00.890 --> 00:42:03.500
So one of my worries earlier on was that,

00:42:03.500 --> 00:42:05.510
well, just because they're
indefinitely causal

00:42:05.510 --> 00:42:09.050
or causally ordered, or even in a...

00:42:09.050 --> 00:42:11.330
Is not necessarily the same thing as being

00:42:11.330 --> 00:42:14.500
in a superposition of A causes B

00:42:14.500 --> 00:42:17.970
just as much as B causes A, if you will.

00:42:17.970 --> 00:42:20.900
But this proposed
experiment does seem to be

00:42:20.900 --> 00:42:25.343
able to discriminate
between what I might call

00:42:25.343 --> 00:42:29.093
a genuine superposition
of causal processes.

00:42:30.660 --> 00:42:32.343
And that's truly weird.

00:42:33.680 --> 00:42:36.820
On the efficacy front, they do find that

00:42:38.040 --> 00:42:39.954
putting detectors in superpositions

00:42:39.954 --> 00:42:43.473
in this way allows them
to be more effective,

00:42:44.650 --> 00:42:47.920
to more effectively perform
entanglement harvesting.

00:42:47.920 --> 00:42:49.660
So just as with the quantum circuit,

00:42:49.660 --> 00:42:53.480
it was found that a
superposition of causal states

00:42:53.480 --> 00:42:56.470
was more effective for
a certain computation,

00:42:56.470 --> 00:42:58.750
here they're finding that
entanglement harvesting,

00:42:58.750 --> 00:43:02.170
or just the probing of the field
for different correlations,

00:43:02.170 --> 00:43:05.200
is more effective when you're dealing with

00:43:05.200 --> 00:43:07.540
superposition of detectors, as opposed to

00:43:09.060 --> 00:43:11.190
pure states of detectors.

00:43:11.190 --> 00:43:14.430
Okay, that's the physics,

00:43:14.430 --> 00:43:16.690
it's all pretty brand-spanking new.

00:43:16.690 --> 00:43:18.053
What can we say about it?

00:43:19.930 --> 00:43:22.030
Here are some things I was thinking about.

00:43:23.760 --> 00:43:26.890
If we think about the proposal made by

00:43:26.890 --> 00:43:29.430
some of the physicists,
that causality might be

00:43:29.430 --> 00:43:32.823
just another aspect of
the uncertainty relations,

00:43:34.020 --> 00:43:37.460
it's uncertain because causality
is ultimately redefined

00:43:37.460 --> 00:43:39.720
in terms of variables at the lower level

00:43:39.720 --> 00:43:43.270
and those variables are
susceptible to uncertainty

00:43:43.270 --> 00:43:46.563
because of quantum mechanics, right?

00:43:49.416 --> 00:43:50.720
Okay, maybe.

00:43:50.720 --> 00:43:53.030
But that doesn't seem to
be what the quantum switch

00:43:53.030 --> 00:43:56.050
or the superpositions
of particle detectors

00:43:56.050 --> 00:43:58.550
in a quantum field are seeing.

00:43:58.550 --> 00:44:00.630
It's not that they're seeing uncertainty

00:44:00.630 --> 00:44:05.050
in the ordering of processes

00:44:05.050 --> 00:44:08.520
it's that they're measuring superpositions

00:44:08.520 --> 00:44:11.963
of well-defined causal processes.

00:44:13.340 --> 00:44:16.870
So I'm not sure that
what benefit it might be

00:44:16.870 --> 00:44:19.540
to think about causality as analogous

00:44:19.540 --> 00:44:22.940
to uncertain properties
like position, momentum,

00:44:22.940 --> 00:44:24.083
velocity, and so on.

00:44:25.270 --> 00:44:27.960
A further problem is
that the interpretation

00:44:27.960 --> 00:44:31.133
of the uncertainty relations
is somewhat debatable.

00:44:32.560 --> 00:44:35.990
I mean, there are limits
on whether you can assign

00:44:35.990 --> 00:44:40.990
well-defined values to a
system before measurements.

00:44:41.120 --> 00:44:43.160
And those limits are
described by, for instance,

00:44:43.160 --> 00:44:45.583
the Kockin Specker Theorem.

00:44:47.030 --> 00:44:49.140
But there are ways that you might recover

00:44:49.140 --> 00:44:51.600
a sort of epistemic or
operational understanding

00:44:51.600 --> 00:44:54.380
of uncertainty, where
it has to do not with

00:44:54.380 --> 00:44:56.880
the nature of the world itself.

00:44:56.880 --> 00:45:01.880
The world itself is just
defined perfectly clearly,

00:45:03.720 --> 00:45:05.790
all the states are
definite states, and so on,

00:45:05.790 --> 00:45:10.090
and it's just that by doing measurements

00:45:10.090 --> 00:45:13.400
on a quantum system introduce disturbances

00:45:13.400 --> 00:45:16.000
of a kind that lead to uncertainty

00:45:16.000 --> 00:45:19.083
in various properties of the system.

00:45:20.880 --> 00:45:22.500
But I'm wondering if there's room to make

00:45:22.500 --> 00:45:25.130
sort of a more metaphysical
or ontological claim here,

00:45:25.130 --> 00:45:26.897
that if you do understand
the uncertainty relations,

00:45:26.897 --> 00:45:29.130
that's saying something
about the way the world is,

00:45:29.130 --> 00:45:31.390
and not just how we measure the world.

00:45:31.390 --> 00:45:33.600
Or if you understand the
way we measure the world

00:45:33.600 --> 00:45:36.690
to be a part of the way the world is,

00:45:36.690 --> 00:45:39.600
maybe measurement just
is defined as weekly as

00:45:39.600 --> 00:45:42.060
an interaction, any sort of interaction.

00:45:42.060 --> 00:45:45.350
Interactions are happening all
the time, all over the place.

00:45:45.350 --> 00:45:47.430
What is the nature of those interactions?

00:45:47.430 --> 00:45:49.990
I'm not sure how we would ever determine

00:45:49.990 --> 00:45:54.840
whether ICO makes
legitimate an interpretation

00:45:54.840 --> 00:45:59.740
of causality as fuzzy, or
is uncertain in this way.

00:45:59.740 --> 00:46:01.340
But I wanna think more about it.

00:46:02.600 --> 00:46:05.690
What seems more plausible and, in fact,

00:46:05.690 --> 00:46:07.420
quantitatively plausible, as well,

00:46:07.420 --> 00:46:10.010
is that we might be able
to come up with theorems

00:46:10.010 --> 00:46:12.840
for measuring or gauging the quantumness

00:46:13.974 --> 00:46:17.183
of relationships between systems.

00:46:18.740 --> 00:46:22.200
And quantum correlations, I
tend to read as entanglement.

00:46:22.200 --> 00:46:24.790
I think that's what's meant
by quantum correlations,

00:46:24.790 --> 00:46:26.050
entanglement.

00:46:26.050 --> 00:46:30.710
So in this way, we can use
these bounds on causality

00:46:30.710 --> 00:46:33.960
to understand more about
entanglement relations

00:46:33.960 --> 00:46:35.930
among different kinds of systems,

00:46:35.930 --> 00:46:38.150
just in the same way that we've measured

00:46:38.150 --> 00:46:41.560
spacial entanglement using Bell tests,

00:46:41.560 --> 00:46:46.210
or maybe the way that we
look at temporal correlations

00:46:46.210 --> 00:46:50.180
and gauge the quantumness
of a system through time

00:46:50.180 --> 00:46:52.850
using Leggett-Garg inequalities.

00:46:52.850 --> 00:46:54.560
So it's been proposed
by, I think Brookner was

00:46:54.560 --> 00:46:59.560
one of these physicists,
that it looks like causality

00:47:00.230 --> 00:47:04.660
that a process, and not just
the spatial-temporal location

00:47:04.660 --> 00:47:07.390
of events, but that a series of events,

00:47:07.390 --> 00:47:10.660
might also have bounds that describe

00:47:10.660 --> 00:47:12.713
entanglement thresholds, as it were.

00:47:15.860 --> 00:47:19.880
There is a further question
which I want to explore,

00:47:19.880 --> 00:47:21.930
which is whether causality is recoverable

00:47:23.250 --> 00:47:25.963
in causally-neutral formulations of QM.

00:47:26.970 --> 00:47:29.730
So these causally-neutral
formulations do exist.

00:47:29.730 --> 00:47:30.960
They're out there.

00:47:30.960 --> 00:47:35.750
But whether they're
realizable in some way,

00:47:35.750 --> 00:47:37.510
and whether the kind of causality,

00:47:37.510 --> 00:47:42.080
the notion of causality that
is recoverable from them is...

00:47:42.080 --> 00:47:43.270
Well, what kind is it?

00:47:43.270 --> 00:47:46.000
Is it merely a descriptive
kind of causality?

00:47:46.000 --> 00:47:50.000
Is it a loose ordering of events

00:47:50.000 --> 00:47:51.820
where you have certain probability bounds,

00:47:51.820 --> 00:47:56.050
like conditional probabilities
that, given this event,

00:47:56.050 --> 00:47:58.940
the probability that
this other event happened

00:47:58.940 --> 00:48:00.900
prior to it as such and such?

00:48:00.900 --> 00:48:03.990
Okay, whether those are
the kinds of causal notions

00:48:03.990 --> 00:48:07.110
that emerge from
causally-neutral formulations

00:48:07.110 --> 00:48:09.930
of quantum theory, or whether it is

00:48:09.930 --> 00:48:13.490
some robust sense of
causality that there's really,

00:48:13.490 --> 00:48:18.490
as Norton would put it,
sort of a factual constraint

00:48:18.640 --> 00:48:22.620
that's being imposed upon
the systems we're measuring

00:48:22.620 --> 00:48:24.010
that is causal in nature,

00:48:24.010 --> 00:48:26.580
yet doesn't exist at the very bottom level

00:48:26.580 --> 00:48:29.453
where we have indefinite causal ordering.

00:48:31.240 --> 00:48:34.443
And then the last sort of
physics-y speculative point,

00:48:35.900 --> 00:48:40.500
which I won't go into, is
just as Bell's inequalities

00:48:40.500 --> 00:48:42.730
and perhaps Leggett-Garg inequalities,

00:48:42.730 --> 00:48:45.710
but to a lesser extent, but
certainly Bell's inequalities

00:48:45.710 --> 00:48:47.850
carved up the interpretation space

00:48:47.850 --> 00:48:50.220
for non-relativistic quantum mechanics

00:48:50.220 --> 00:48:52.360
in a very different sort of way, right?

00:48:52.360 --> 00:48:57.220
It made more clear the
goal of a de Broglie-Bohm

00:48:57.220 --> 00:48:59.113
contextual hidden variables theory.

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:02.600
This is what the theory
needed to do to recover

00:49:04.260 --> 00:49:08.973
empirical data, yet account for causality

00:49:11.390 --> 00:49:14.010
because de Broglie-Bohm, they want to have

00:49:14.010 --> 00:49:16.190
sort of classical processes
in the background,

00:49:16.190 --> 00:49:19.110
or recover classical
processes the level of,

00:49:19.110 --> 00:49:20.810
I don't know, the universe, maybe.

00:49:22.060 --> 00:49:25.100
Right, so maybe in the same way,

00:49:25.100 --> 00:49:28.640
if we come up with causality inequalities

00:49:28.640 --> 00:49:31.660
based on indefinite causal ordering,

00:49:31.660 --> 00:49:34.880
that will shed some
light on or re-carve up

00:49:34.880 --> 00:49:37.750
the interpretation space
in an interesting way.

00:49:37.750 --> 00:49:41.160
But I suspect that the right move is not

00:49:41.160 --> 00:49:43.570
to re-carve up the space, but in fact...

00:49:44.430 --> 00:49:46.360
Oh, this is a general conclusion,

00:49:46.360 --> 00:49:48.323
but I'll go to it now.

00:49:49.830 --> 00:49:51.890
No, I won't. (laughs)

00:49:51.890 --> 00:49:54.360
Hold on, just keep it in mind.

00:49:54.360 --> 00:49:57.610
I suspect that it's just more impetus

00:49:57.610 --> 00:50:00.700
for ditching causality
as a fundamental aspect

00:50:00.700 --> 00:50:03.670
of our interpretation of the
world in the first place.

00:50:03.670 --> 00:50:07.501
So if you've built, as some
Bohmians, for instance,

00:50:07.501 --> 00:50:10.620
and sorry I'm picking on
Bohmians today, most days,

00:50:10.620 --> 00:50:13.230
if the Bohmians have...

00:50:13.230 --> 00:50:16.900
Their motivation for developing
their pilot wave theory

00:50:16.900 --> 00:50:18.860
or their potential hidden variables theory

00:50:18.860 --> 00:50:23.430
was to recover, at the macro
scale, classical behavior,

00:50:23.430 --> 00:50:25.940
they want their systems tracing out

00:50:25.940 --> 00:50:27.923
quasi-neutronean trajectories,

00:50:29.540 --> 00:50:32.400
they want the world particle
to be moving deterministically,

00:50:32.400 --> 00:50:34.840
that means that the prior state is a cause

00:50:34.840 --> 00:50:37.543
for the later state, and so on.

00:50:38.690 --> 00:50:40.900
So a large motivation for
that interpretation is

00:50:40.900 --> 00:50:45.350
to recover classical notions,
among them causality,

00:50:45.350 --> 00:50:48.453
arguably, in which case,

00:50:49.500 --> 00:50:51.010
maybe this is just another reason

00:50:51.010 --> 00:50:53.070
to sort of avoid that interpretation.

00:50:53.070 --> 00:50:53.903
Okay.

00:50:55.660 --> 00:50:58.310
Another point to make very quickly is that

00:50:58.310 --> 00:51:02.530
a lot of discussion about
relativistic causality in physics,

00:51:02.530 --> 00:51:05.010
and, as far as I know, in
the philosophical literature

00:51:05.010 --> 00:51:08.840
has focused on signaling and
the bounds placed on signaling

00:51:08.840 --> 00:51:11.510
by no-signaling theorems and so on.

00:51:11.510 --> 00:51:13.980
But I think that these ICO processes

00:51:13.980 --> 00:51:18.980
and the Henderson et al
detectors and superpositions

00:51:20.200 --> 00:51:23.290
in the field, I think these
are methods of understanding

00:51:23.290 --> 00:51:26.090
or of approaching the question
of relativistic causality

00:51:27.317 --> 00:51:29.250
that are not primarily about signaling.

00:51:29.250 --> 00:51:31.680
So it might be just different
avenue for thinking about

00:51:31.680 --> 00:51:34.443
relativistic causality, broadly speaking.

00:51:35.300 --> 00:51:36.500
How are we on time?

00:51:36.500 --> 00:51:37.380
Oh, fantastic.

00:51:37.380 --> 00:51:38.483
I shall now wrap up.

00:51:39.870 --> 00:51:41.110
I've already mentioned

00:51:41.110 --> 00:51:43.670
some of the relativistic consequences,

00:51:43.670 --> 00:51:47.830
namely that it gives us more
specific details about...

00:51:50.480 --> 00:51:54.053
It gives us larger regions
for entanglement harvesting.

00:51:55.330 --> 00:51:58.060
So we just learn more
about the quantum field

00:51:58.060 --> 00:52:02.860
when we couple it to superposed
detectors in this way.

00:52:02.860 --> 00:52:04.140
But we also asked the question,

00:52:04.140 --> 00:52:06.140
and I heard Henderson at a talk she gave,

00:52:06.140 --> 00:52:07.927
she ended the talk by saying,

00:52:07.927 --> 00:52:10.287
"It looks like we're dealing with

00:52:10.287 --> 00:52:13.157
"physically-superposed detectors that have

00:52:13.157 --> 00:52:18.157
"a real effect on the
probing of entanglement."

00:52:18.250 --> 00:52:19.880
What does this mean?

00:52:19.880 --> 00:52:21.760
What does this mean for how we construct

00:52:21.760 --> 00:52:23.453
future quantized theories?

00:52:25.040 --> 00:52:28.180
I think she was asking, "What
is the metaphysics of this?"

00:52:28.180 --> 00:52:29.013
Right?

00:52:29.013 --> 00:52:30.310
Well, let's figure it out.

00:52:32.930 --> 00:52:34.680
I've mentioned, also,
that it motivates attempts

00:52:34.680 --> 00:52:38.440
to develop a theory of quantum gravity

00:52:38.440 --> 00:52:40.160
or any kind of quantum theory

00:52:40.160 --> 00:52:41.540
that is background independent.

00:52:41.540 --> 00:52:44.050
So it doesn't assume going
in that there's a set

00:52:44.050 --> 00:52:46.540
space-time manifold, or that
there's causal structure

00:52:46.540 --> 00:52:51.540
embedded in the background, okay?

00:52:52.330 --> 00:52:53.760
So maybe...

00:52:54.950 --> 00:52:57.030
Ooh, that was supposed to...

00:52:57.030 --> 00:52:58.610
I didn't finish editing that point.

00:52:58.610 --> 00:53:00.300
But go on to three, look down at three,

00:53:00.300 --> 00:53:03.380
don't pay attention to two. (laughs)

00:53:03.380 --> 00:53:05.470
Oreshkov and others have suggested that

00:53:05.470 --> 00:53:08.920
if causality, like space-time,
should be considered

00:53:08.920 --> 00:53:12.580
dynamically emergent, I mean,
what we see at the macro scale

00:53:12.580 --> 00:53:14.940
as clear causal connections,

00:53:14.940 --> 00:53:19.940
do not exist in that definite
way at the micro level,

00:53:21.600 --> 00:53:25.630
then perhaps we can
start building up a story

00:53:25.630 --> 00:53:28.970
about the emergence of those more concrete

00:53:28.970 --> 00:53:31.870
or more classical causal structures.

00:53:31.870 --> 00:53:34.280
And maybe, I suggest,
those are dynamical stories

00:53:34.280 --> 00:53:37.683
that involve, for instance,
quantum decoherence processes,

00:53:39.360 --> 00:53:42.770
which can effectively
damp those superpositions

00:53:42.770 --> 00:53:45.820
so that even though they are
physically superpositions,

00:53:45.820 --> 00:53:47.670
they nevertheless are only measurable

00:53:48.710 --> 00:53:51.920
by some other probe, by
an environment, by us,

00:53:51.920 --> 00:53:53.850
by a lab, by Alice, by Bob

00:53:53.850 --> 00:53:55.930
as being effectively
an Eigen state, right?

00:53:55.930 --> 00:53:57.473
So not in a superposition.

00:53:58.930 --> 00:54:02.350
And it might even support
canonical approaches

00:54:02.350 --> 00:54:05.310
to quantum gravity over
co-variant approaches

00:54:05.310 --> 00:54:07.840
because canonical approaches,

00:54:07.840 --> 00:54:10.900
because the Willard equation
doesn't evolve in time,

00:54:10.900 --> 00:54:13.070
time has to come from somewhere.

00:54:13.070 --> 00:54:15.330
And there's some work, like Moravia et al

00:54:15.330 --> 00:54:17.030
has this interesting paper where

00:54:17.030 --> 00:54:20.930
he and his team derive,
say that even if you have

00:54:20.930 --> 00:54:23.890
a static macro system or composite system,

00:54:23.890 --> 00:54:25.790
if you carve it up into subsystems

00:54:25.790 --> 00:54:28.230
that are entangled with each
other in the right sort of way,

00:54:28.230 --> 00:54:29.890
they can be evolving in time

00:54:29.890 --> 00:54:32.700
and you still have a composite
state that is static.

00:54:32.700 --> 00:54:35.030
Well, that's exactly the
sort of thing you want

00:54:35.030 --> 00:54:37.300
for a canonical approach
to quantum gravity,

00:54:37.300 --> 00:54:40.023
where the real equation
is not evolving in time.

00:54:40.900 --> 00:54:44.856
But again, it's possible that
a project like Moravia's,

00:54:44.856 --> 00:54:49.325
that has to do with recovering
time from entanglement,

00:54:49.325 --> 00:54:51.960
is possible in terms of causal processes

00:54:51.960 --> 00:54:55.123
as emergent from entanglement in that way.

00:54:56.280 --> 00:54:59.630
Okay, so the specific thesis, just,

00:54:59.630 --> 00:55:01.420
it seems that this physics,

00:55:01.420 --> 00:55:04.500
just the fact that there
are a lot of people studying

00:55:04.500 --> 00:55:07.200
indefinite causal processes
in a variety of ways,

00:55:07.200 --> 00:55:10.910
and not just operationally,
but as what it could tell us

00:55:10.910 --> 00:55:12.860
about fundamental features of the world

00:55:12.860 --> 00:55:15.233
like entanglement in quantum field theory,

00:55:16.470 --> 00:55:20.890
that causality either just
isn't a part of the world

00:55:20.890 --> 00:55:23.760
at the lower level, or if
it is, it's indefinite,

00:55:23.760 --> 00:55:25.550
but we should become clear about

00:55:25.550 --> 00:55:27.190
in what sense it's indefinite

00:55:27.190 --> 00:55:29.670
and if there are bounds
on how definite it is

00:55:29.670 --> 00:55:30.793
or indefinite it is.

00:55:31.710 --> 00:55:36.430
And given that we do use
cause at higher levels,

00:55:36.430 --> 00:55:38.530
what is the nature of
these causal processes,

00:55:38.530 --> 00:55:40.580
given that there's clear evidence that

00:55:40.580 --> 00:55:43.083
if they are emergent,
which they probably are,

00:55:44.880 --> 00:55:48.380
how does that emergence
process happen, right?

00:55:48.380 --> 00:55:51.210
There's nothing causal
at the fundamental level,

00:55:51.210 --> 00:55:53.253
whence cause at this higher level.

00:55:55.580 --> 00:55:56.480
Right.

00:55:56.480 --> 00:55:59.640
And then, of course, looking
at the relativistic case

00:55:59.640 --> 00:56:01.840
of Henderson et al, we can understand more

00:56:01.840 --> 00:56:04.270
about entanglements in
relativistic scenarios

00:56:04.270 --> 00:56:09.270
by studying using indefinite
causal ordering as a tool

00:56:09.400 --> 00:56:12.380
to probe so it's not
just that we've measured

00:56:12.380 --> 00:56:13.280
indefinite causal ordering,

00:56:13.280 --> 00:56:15.870
it's that we're now using
indefinite causal ordering

00:56:15.870 --> 00:56:18.900
to understand other parts
of the physical universe.

00:56:18.900 --> 00:56:19.863
That's pretty cool.

00:56:21.700 --> 00:56:23.053
Ending where I began.

00:56:23.920 --> 00:56:25.680
If we look at the three major camps

00:56:25.680 --> 00:56:27.070
for metaphysics of causation,

00:56:27.070 --> 00:56:28.690
it looks like causal fundamentalists have

00:56:28.690 --> 00:56:31.533
an even harder task, given ICO.

00:56:33.360 --> 00:56:37.450
So for instance, the
Bohmians often say that

00:56:37.450 --> 00:56:41.170
we require a primitive
ontology that has a law,

00:56:41.170 --> 00:56:45.070
that's a causal law, and
the Bohemian world particle

00:56:45.070 --> 00:56:47.303
evolves according to that causal law.

00:56:51.490 --> 00:56:54.590
That seems less attractive
to me, given ICO,

00:56:54.590 --> 00:56:57.510
and sort of all this active research.

00:56:57.510 --> 00:56:58.500
And the eliminativists,

00:56:58.500 --> 00:57:00.350
there could be serious problems, too,

00:57:01.340 --> 00:57:03.550
because if causal talk, like Norton wants,

00:57:03.550 --> 00:57:06.220
is only allowed in virtue of
its being ultimately reduceable

00:57:06.220 --> 00:57:09.160
to some non-causal micro theory,

00:57:09.160 --> 00:57:11.140
well, what if it's not
just there's no cause

00:57:11.140 --> 00:57:13.040
down there in this...

00:57:13.040 --> 00:57:13.873
Or that it...

00:57:13.873 --> 00:57:16.750
But that it's sort of
entangled causal processes.

00:57:16.750 --> 00:57:18.140
There have been arguments already

00:57:18.140 --> 00:57:20.620
in the non-relativistic
literature that you can't have

00:57:20.620 --> 00:57:23.270
reduction onto a base that's entangled

00:57:23.270 --> 00:57:26.860
because entanglement is sort
of a holistic property, right?

00:57:26.860 --> 00:57:29.710
Entanglement relations
cannot be further reduced

00:57:29.710 --> 00:57:32.923
because the parts are something
different than the whole.

00:57:33.830 --> 00:57:35.530
Hello, hello.

00:57:35.530 --> 00:57:38.250
And then finally, I think
this comes out on top

00:57:38.250 --> 00:57:40.460
for the non-fundamentalists.

00:57:40.460 --> 00:57:41.860
They seem to be safe on their claim

00:57:41.860 --> 00:57:43.720
that causality does not exist

00:57:43.720 --> 00:57:46.160
in the fundamental makeup of the universe,

00:57:46.160 --> 00:57:48.120
but it seems like we still have

00:57:49.670 --> 00:57:54.530
important uses for
recovering causal processes

00:57:54.530 --> 00:57:57.560
or causal models, like
the quantum Markovian

00:57:57.560 --> 00:58:00.530
causal processes at the higher level.

00:58:00.530 --> 00:58:02.603
Quantum Markovian models, rather.

00:58:03.970 --> 00:58:06.580
But I think the task may
have become slightly harder,

00:58:06.580 --> 00:58:10.770
given ICO, because
there's maybe a larger gap

00:58:10.770 --> 00:58:13.013
between the fundamental
level and where it is

00:58:13.013 --> 00:58:15.690
that causality comes in and is useful

00:58:15.690 --> 00:58:18.170
to our modeling processes.

00:58:18.170 --> 00:58:19.750
Okay, so a lot.

00:58:19.750 --> 00:58:20.710
Very speculative.

00:58:20.710 --> 00:58:22.310
Can't wait to have a discussion with you.

00:58:22.310 --> 00:58:24.030
Here are very brief references.

00:58:24.030 --> 00:58:26.693
Thank you very much. (laughs)

00:58:30.510 --> 00:58:34.610
- I thank you so much, Elise,
for this wonderful talk.

00:58:34.610 --> 00:58:36.393
Much to think about.

00:58:37.820 --> 00:58:41.250
And before I open up
the floor for questions,

00:58:41.250 --> 00:58:45.110
I wanted to acknowledge the fact that

00:58:45.110 --> 00:58:48.410
we are delighted, Elise, that
you are part of our core team

00:58:48.410 --> 00:58:50.120
