01:03:44.760 --> 01:03:47.023
- Yes, yes, Shankar, please do.

01:03:48.213 --> 01:03:52.060
- [Shankar] So I think the
subject is very difficult,

01:03:52.060 --> 01:03:54.480
but I do feel that if somebody
could explain it to me,

01:03:54.480 --> 01:03:56.003
it is our speaker today.

01:03:56.930 --> 01:03:58.370
- Oh wow.
- 'Cause the part

01:03:58.370 --> 01:04:01.450
that I knew, I could see
how well it was explained,

01:04:01.450 --> 01:04:03.940
but lots of it I don't know,

01:04:03.940 --> 01:04:06.150
I'm just a Copenhagen hack.

01:04:06.150 --> 01:04:07.578
I sort of know

01:04:07.578 --> 01:04:08.884
(Priya laughing)

01:04:08.884 --> 01:04:10.200
the standard way to do it.

01:04:10.200 --> 01:04:12.950
In my world, measuring
instruments that are

01:04:12.950 --> 01:04:16.070
for different size from
the measured objects,

01:04:16.070 --> 01:04:17.870
and a very clear distinction between

01:04:18.874 --> 01:04:21.770
the macroscopic world needed
to define measurements.

01:04:21.770 --> 01:04:23.300
I think, in this study,

01:04:23.300 --> 01:04:25.400
you're reaching a domain
where they get blurred,

01:04:25.400 --> 01:04:26.663
where they get small.

01:04:28.535 --> 01:04:30.896
So their issues are never talked about.

01:04:30.896 --> 01:04:33.410
But as I said, if I want to
learn what those issues are,

01:04:33.410 --> 01:04:34.740
this was a very good introduction.

01:04:34.740 --> 01:04:37.480
So I gotta thank her,
Elise, for doing that.

01:04:37.480 --> 01:04:38.760
- I'm really honored.

01:04:38.760 --> 01:04:40.930
Can I ask you a question
while I've got you?

01:04:40.930 --> 01:04:42.560
- Yes, I was just gonna say.

01:04:42.560 --> 01:04:44.630
- As a philosopher of physics,

01:04:44.630 --> 01:04:47.278
it may not be the one you
want, but as a philosopher,

01:04:47.278 --> 01:04:48.613
I'm a student of John Howard's,

01:04:48.613 --> 01:04:49.446
who was famous, among other things,

01:04:49.446 --> 01:04:52.200
for writing about the myth of
the Copenhagen interpretation,

01:04:52.200 --> 01:04:54.570
sort of historically.

01:04:54.570 --> 01:04:56.660
It was an interpretation
Heisenberg came up with

01:04:56.660 --> 01:04:59.910
after World War II that
aligned more closely with Bohr.

01:04:59.910 --> 01:05:02.950
Bohr emerged from World War II as a hero,

01:05:02.950 --> 01:05:05.490
advocating Glasnost, then
meeting with Churchill.

01:05:05.490 --> 01:05:08.400
And well, he did work on
the Manhattan Project,

01:05:08.400 --> 01:05:12.823
but he was, I don't
know, careful about it.

01:05:14.310 --> 01:05:16.013
He was very conscientious.

01:05:17.180 --> 01:05:19.890
Heisenberg did not
emerge from World War II

01:05:19.890 --> 01:05:21.920
with a similar reputation.

01:05:21.920 --> 01:05:23.390
And so, it was...

01:05:24.330 --> 01:05:25.670
He made it up, okay?

01:05:25.670 --> 01:05:29.027
So if you historically
study Heisenberg's view

01:05:29.027 --> 01:05:30.970
and Paolie's view and Yordan's view

01:05:30.970 --> 01:05:33.240
and Einstein's view and Schrodinger's view

01:05:33.240 --> 01:05:35.730
in the period 1926 to 1935,

01:05:35.730 --> 01:05:37.720
which I have a book coming out on that.

01:05:37.720 --> 01:05:39.910
They have very rich and very
nuanced different views.

01:05:39.910 --> 01:05:41.730
So here's my question.

01:05:41.730 --> 01:05:44.130
Many physicists-assigned
counters still claim

01:05:44.130 --> 01:05:47.030
to be Copenhagen people, but they're...

01:05:47.030 --> 01:05:48.540
What does Copenhagen mean for you?

01:05:48.540 --> 01:05:51.140
That there's a distinct classical realm?

01:05:51.140 --> 01:05:53.420
Or that measurement processes produce

01:05:53.420 --> 01:05:54.810
truly definite outcomes,

01:05:54.810 --> 01:05:58.210
not just apparently or
effectively definite outcomes?

01:05:58.210 --> 01:05:59.570
- [Shankar] Yeah, I imagine a world.

01:05:59.570 --> 01:06:01.970
Look, you got experiments
going on all the time.

01:06:01.970 --> 01:06:04.280
In CERN, they're measuring particles.

01:06:04.280 --> 01:06:06.070
There's a probability distribution

01:06:06.070 --> 01:06:08.656
for where the particle will land.

01:06:08.656 --> 01:06:09.489
- Right.

01:06:09.489 --> 01:06:10.860
- [Shankar] And if it
detected a place or an angle

01:06:10.860 --> 01:06:14.470
and you vary the angle, there's
a function that's predicted.

01:06:14.470 --> 01:06:15.830
And that seems to work.

01:06:15.830 --> 01:06:18.360
We seem to know what we mean
when we measure the pion

01:06:18.360 --> 01:06:20.130
in this detector.

01:06:20.130 --> 01:06:22.660
That's the level at which I
understand quantum mechanics,

01:06:22.660 --> 01:06:24.143
the level at which it works.

01:06:24.984 --> 01:06:25.817
- Okay.

01:06:25.817 --> 01:06:27.400
- [Shankar] But there, detectors
that you would, in fact,

01:06:27.400 --> 01:06:30.490
particular experimentalist
never fail to show you

01:06:30.490 --> 01:06:32.520
how big they are compared
to a human being.

01:06:32.520 --> 01:06:34.020
All the pictures have it.

01:06:34.020 --> 01:06:37.470
You would detect it in a tiny
person standing next to them.

01:06:37.470 --> 01:06:40.210
That world, the problems
you say will not arise.

01:06:40.210 --> 01:06:42.060
You can never put two of those big guys

01:06:42.060 --> 01:06:43.253
in a superposed state.

01:06:44.202 --> 01:06:45.035
- Right.

01:06:45.035 --> 01:06:47.194
- [Shankar] So I don't have
the issues you are raising.

01:06:47.194 --> 01:06:48.027
These are issues.

01:06:48.027 --> 01:06:49.830
You have to deal with the
issues before they come

01:06:49.830 --> 01:06:50.700
and ruin your life.

01:06:50.700 --> 01:06:54.840
But at the moment, I'm almost
finished with my career,

01:06:54.840 --> 01:06:57.840
so I'm not worried about what'll
happen 15 years from now,

01:06:57.840 --> 01:06:59.723
but these could be dominant issues.

01:07:00.600 --> 01:07:03.880
- Yeah, and so, I mean, this
is brand new stuff for me.

01:07:03.880 --> 01:07:06.210
What I have mostly done,
aside from the historical

01:07:06.210 --> 01:07:09.080
and foundational work
is decoherence studies,

01:07:09.080 --> 01:07:10.490
and looked at sort of that.

01:07:10.490 --> 01:07:13.890
And so, it does seem clear
that, at least in principle,

01:07:13.890 --> 01:07:17.450
there is no well-defined domain.

01:07:17.450 --> 01:07:21.290
I mean, even if you take
the usual limiting theorems,

01:07:21.290 --> 01:07:24.520
like Aaron Fest's theorum
or the Louisville regime

01:07:24.520 --> 01:07:28.090
or whatever, none of these
gives a universal description

01:07:29.300 --> 01:07:31.710
or a universal classification
that slices the world

01:07:31.710 --> 01:07:33.710
where you get just classical
systems on one side

01:07:33.710 --> 01:07:34.550
and quantum on the other,

01:07:34.550 --> 01:07:35.910
whatever those may be.

01:07:35.910 --> 01:07:39.670
You always have problems.

01:07:39.670 --> 01:07:43.570
And so let's just let
everything be quantum (laughs)

01:07:44.780 --> 01:07:49.070
and then let the dynamics
of quantum processes,

01:07:49.070 --> 01:07:50.590
like decoherence-

01:07:50.590 --> 01:07:51.884
- [Shankar] That's true.

01:07:51.884 --> 01:07:53.840
- Tell us why we see
effectively classical states

01:07:53.840 --> 01:07:57.250
at mezzoscopic and macroscopic scales.

01:07:57.250 --> 01:07:58.083
- [Shankar] Right.

01:07:58.083 --> 01:08:01.320
And there are also people
at Yale, like Jack Harris,

01:08:01.320 --> 01:08:03.080
who are flirting with the dividing line

01:08:03.080 --> 01:08:05.290
between classical and quantum.

01:08:05.290 --> 01:08:08.460
They got mechanical objects, which are,

01:08:08.460 --> 01:08:11.463
I think, the largest one in
which you can see superposition.

01:08:13.510 --> 01:08:16.530
So they are cantilibra oscillators,

01:08:16.530 --> 01:08:18.840
which can be in a superposed state.

01:08:18.840 --> 01:08:20.050
- Right.

01:08:20.050 --> 01:08:20.883
- [Shankar] It's a big enough thing that

01:08:20.883 --> 01:08:21.950
you could probably see in a microscope,

01:08:21.950 --> 01:08:24.500
but yet it is really
in a superposed state.

01:08:24.500 --> 01:08:27.990
But I thought everyone agrees
that that is challenging,

01:08:27.990 --> 01:08:29.720
but it's gonna be a long way from getting

01:08:29.720 --> 01:08:33.800
anything substantial
in a superposed state.

01:08:33.800 --> 01:08:36.030
- And that's partly the game too, right?

01:08:36.030 --> 01:08:39.370
Because this is clearly a
problem for quantum computing,

01:08:39.370 --> 01:08:40.480
where you want to shield your...

01:08:40.480 --> 01:08:43.230
You want to maintain the
integrity of your qubits.

01:08:43.230 --> 01:08:45.540
You can only do that by
maintaining coherence.

01:08:45.540 --> 01:08:49.193
But decoherence is so damn
defective and so fast,

01:08:49.193 --> 01:08:51.523
that this is a real engineering problem.

01:08:52.510 --> 01:08:53.880
But if we're concerned with,

01:08:53.880 --> 01:08:55.689
if we're interested in the metaphysics,

01:08:55.689 --> 01:08:57.860
the fact that we're
trying to shield systems

01:08:57.860 --> 01:08:59.720
from losing this property indicates

01:08:59.720 --> 01:09:03.240
something about the way
nature prefers it, as it were,

01:09:03.240 --> 01:09:06.433
in sort of this quantum
entangled situation.

01:09:07.980 --> 01:09:08.813
Yeah.

01:09:08.813 --> 01:09:09.646
- [Shankar] Yes, I agree.

01:09:09.646 --> 01:09:12.270
I think I shouldn't monopolize your time,

01:09:12.270 --> 01:09:14.710
so thanks for taking my comment

01:09:14.710 --> 01:09:18.523
and maybe I should let
other people talk to you.

01:09:19.600 --> 01:09:20.720
- [Priya] Thanks, Shankar.

01:09:20.720 --> 01:09:22.660
- [Shankar] Okay, I'm
going to mute myself.

01:09:22.660 --> 01:09:27.610
- Yeah, so there, I think,
Peter, did you have a question

01:09:27.610 --> 01:09:30.123
