or retro-causation, and
you could understand

01:10:07.720 --> 01:10:11.740
what you described, or
certainly entanglements.

01:10:11.740 --> 01:10:14.420
There were ideas about
expanding entanglement,

01:10:14.420 --> 01:10:16.383
in terms of retro causality.

01:10:18.510 --> 01:10:20.050
You never mentioned the term,

01:10:20.050 --> 01:10:23.800
but what you said seems
to be compatible with that

01:10:23.800 --> 01:10:25.700
in some cases.

01:10:25.700 --> 01:10:29.540
So what is the connection

01:10:29.540 --> 01:10:32.740
between what you said and
what those interpreters

01:10:32.740 --> 01:10:35.620
of quantum mechanics,
like say retro-causation,

01:10:35.620 --> 01:10:36.783
as an explanation?

01:10:38.160 --> 01:10:39.350
- Well, Yemima, thanks.

01:10:39.350 --> 01:10:40.790
I hope that's something we can talk about

01:10:40.790 --> 01:10:42.080
at more length tomorrow,

01:10:42.080 --> 01:10:45.498
and I sort of intentionally
left it out today.

01:10:45.498 --> 01:10:48.940
I waved at it from a
distance when I talked about

01:10:48.940 --> 01:10:51.620
the different, like maybe
that there's some...

01:10:52.600 --> 01:10:53.920
There's the lack of clarity about

01:10:53.920 --> 01:10:55.940
whether we're talking
about causal processes

01:10:55.940 --> 01:10:57.180
that are causally ordered,

01:10:57.180 --> 01:10:58.840
and it's just unclear.

01:10:58.840 --> 01:11:00.040
Of course-
- Right.

01:11:00.040 --> 01:11:02.500
- A superposition of a
legitimate A cause B,

01:11:02.500 --> 01:11:05.710
but then B cause A, and
retro causality would only

01:11:05.710 --> 01:11:10.310
really apply to part of
one of those situations.

01:11:10.310 --> 01:11:12.910
And so, in a sense, if we can sort out

01:11:14.080 --> 01:11:17.640
what a superposition of
causal processes really means,

01:11:17.640 --> 01:11:21.500
that will illuminate how
much overlap, if any,

01:11:21.500 --> 01:11:24.620
there is with the retro-causal literature.

01:11:24.620 --> 01:11:27.100
But retro-causality...

01:11:27.100 --> 01:11:29.240
So one of the historical figures

01:11:29.240 --> 01:11:31.050
I've done a lot of work
on is Gretta Herrmann,

01:11:31.050 --> 01:11:33.550
who wrote the greatest and maybe the first

01:11:33.550 --> 01:11:36.800
philosophic treatise on
quantum mechanics, in 1935.

01:11:36.800 --> 01:11:38.300
And she she's a Kantean,

01:11:38.300 --> 01:11:42.530
so she wants to preserve a
fundamental causal principle.

01:11:42.530 --> 01:11:44.900
But she does this by first teasing apart,

01:11:44.900 --> 01:11:46.870
as you well know, Yemima,

01:11:46.870 --> 01:11:49.950
as you do so nicely in the
introduction to your book,

01:11:49.950 --> 01:11:53.450
determination and prediction
is a separate thing,

01:11:53.450 --> 01:11:57.680
and the existence of a
chain of cause and effect.

01:11:57.680 --> 01:12:00.030
And there is a chain of cause and effect,

01:12:00.030 --> 01:12:01.260
she says, in quantum mechanics,

01:12:01.260 --> 01:12:03.830
we just cannot know it beforehand.

01:12:03.830 --> 01:12:06.437
We can know it retrodictively.

01:12:06.437 --> 01:12:08.100
So it's not retrocausality,

01:12:08.100 --> 01:12:10.250
but sort of retrodiction of something

01:12:10.250 --> 01:12:12.173
that already existed in the world,

01:12:13.980 --> 01:12:16.160
is sort of her way of maintaining

01:12:16.160 --> 01:12:18.703
the causal completeness
of quantum mechanics.

01:12:20.040 --> 01:12:22.030
But yeah, I know Hugh
Price and others have done

01:12:22.030 --> 01:12:24.040
really interesting work
on perpetual causality.

01:12:24.040 --> 01:12:27.810
And if a retrocausal
thesis is still required,

01:12:27.810 --> 01:12:30.120
a particular causal ordering of events,

01:12:30.120 --> 01:12:33.950
it's just not in the order
we are used to or experience.

01:12:33.950 --> 01:12:35.800
That's a different thing than saying

01:12:35.800 --> 01:12:39.500
that it's a one of square
root two superposition

01:12:39.500 --> 01:12:41.600
of A caused B and B caused A.

01:12:41.600 --> 01:12:43.520
What does that mean?

01:12:43.520 --> 01:12:45.290
So I hope we can talk
about that more tomorrow,

01:12:45.290 --> 01:12:46.520
and you can teach me some things

01:12:46.520 --> 01:12:50.596
about retrocausality. (laughs)

01:12:50.596 --> 01:12:51.429
- Oh, great.

01:12:51.429 --> 01:12:53.020
So I just wanted to read
out a couple of shout-outs

01:12:53.020 --> 01:12:55.870
before moving on to the next question.

01:12:55.870 --> 01:12:58.030
So Lorenzo Moniani said...

01:12:58.030 --> 01:13:00.460
Has provided a reference on some ideas

01:13:00.460 --> 01:13:02.560
about physics of consciousness.

01:13:02.560 --> 01:13:04.940
He refers to the area of studies called

01:13:04.940 --> 01:13:07.400
the so-called dissipative brain.

01:13:07.400 --> 01:13:11.437
For example, there's
a chapter in a volume,

01:13:11.437 --> 01:13:13.750
"Understanding Brain and Consciousness"

01:13:13.750 --> 01:13:16.667
by Zvitlelo Vitelo.

01:13:17.970 --> 01:13:20.350
And he's given the reference
for anyone who wants it,

01:13:20.350 --> 01:13:21.350
in the chat.

01:13:21.350 --> 01:13:25.210
And Peter Morgan gave a
shout out for Dawn Howard's

01:13:25.210 --> 01:13:27.960
2004 paper pointing out
the difference between

01:13:27.960 --> 01:13:29.760
Bohr and Heisenberg on the nature of...

01:13:29.760 --> 01:13:31.543
That's a wonderful paper.

01:13:32.920 --> 01:13:36.750
And he's mentioning that
it was formative for him.

01:13:36.750 --> 01:13:40.510
It was quite the paper to plow
through, but it's excellent.

01:13:40.510 --> 01:13:42.880
So I think, for those...

01:13:44.518 --> 01:13:46.543
It was another of our regular attendees,

01:13:47.468 --> 01:13:49.190
and fellow astrophysicist,

01:13:49.190 --> 01:13:51.240
he has a very nice question,

01:13:51.240 --> 01:13:53.110
which is sort of where
I was going next, too,

01:13:53.110 --> 01:13:56.397
that he says, "You mentioned decoherence

01:13:56.397 --> 01:13:58.787
"and that your thesis
work was based on it.

01:13:58.787 --> 01:14:00.717
"Can you elaborate a little bit more

01:14:00.717 --> 01:14:04.037
"on how this may have influenced
your developing the ideas,

01:14:04.037 --> 01:14:06.187
"for example, that
you've presented today?"

01:14:07.280 --> 01:14:11.620
- Sure, well... (laughs)

01:14:13.160 --> 01:14:17.120
Decoherence is due to entanglement.

01:14:17.120 --> 01:14:20.410
So there's a deep
mother-daughter relationship

01:14:20.410 --> 01:14:22.830
between entanglement and decoherence.

01:14:22.830 --> 01:14:26.400
And there's also a lot of
philosophical interests,

01:14:26.400 --> 01:14:29.310
but also confusion about
both of these properties.

01:14:29.310 --> 01:14:32.560
Well, excuse me,
entanglement as a property

01:14:32.560 --> 01:14:35.240
that systems might have and degrees of it

01:14:35.240 --> 01:14:37.270
and as it evolves through time,

01:14:37.270 --> 01:14:39.350
and decoherence as a temporal...

01:14:39.350 --> 01:14:40.963
As a dynamical process.

01:14:42.270 --> 01:14:44.870
So I've been thinking for a
long time about entanglements

01:14:44.870 --> 01:14:47.870
in that guise, how it gives rise to

01:14:47.870 --> 01:14:51.160
and how it's not evidence at
the mezzo and macro scale,

01:14:51.160 --> 01:14:52.561
because of...

01:14:52.561 --> 01:14:55.317
A lot of puzzles having
to do with entanglement,

01:14:55.317 --> 01:14:58.373
I think can be given
explanations via decoherence.

01:14:59.269 --> 01:15:00.890
So that's the relationship
between entitlement

01:15:00.890 --> 01:15:02.810
and decoherence.

01:15:02.810 --> 01:15:05.920
But also, of course, these
questions have to do with

01:15:05.920 --> 01:15:08.180
the quantum to classical transition.

01:15:08.180 --> 01:15:12.243
And because I'm interested
in talking to metaphysicists,

01:15:12.243 --> 01:15:13.271
metaphysicians.

01:15:13.271 --> 01:15:14.287
I think they go by metaphysicians,

01:15:14.287 --> 01:15:15.987
but it ought to be metaphysicists.

01:15:17.040 --> 01:15:20.280
I'm interested in understanding ontology,

01:15:20.280 --> 01:15:23.507
but in a way where it captures
our best physics, right?

01:15:23.507 --> 01:15:27.010
And our best physics says that
there's no hard and fast way

01:15:27.010 --> 01:15:30.510
to carve up the universe
into classical and quantum

01:15:30.510 --> 01:15:34.730
or even individual or
closed or isolated systems.

01:15:34.730 --> 01:15:36.130
You were talking about dissipation.

01:15:36.130 --> 01:15:38.547
Well, dissipation is a big problem, right?

01:15:40.220 --> 01:15:43.580
The fact that we can effectively
deal with Eigen states

01:15:43.580 --> 01:15:47.680
or that we effectively
measure with pointers

01:15:47.680 --> 01:15:50.810
a particular value for
our measurement outcome,

01:15:50.810 --> 01:15:53.430
that there is a quantum process underlying

01:15:53.430 --> 01:15:56.580
those classical phenomenon
was really something

01:15:56.580 --> 01:15:57.530
I wanted to understand.

01:15:57.530 --> 01:16:00.740
Now, causality is a little bit higher up

01:16:00.740 --> 01:16:02.930
because it's sort of leaping a step,

01:16:02.930 --> 01:16:04.100
because I wanna think a little bit...

01:16:04.100 --> 01:16:07.100
Entanglement as a process
across space and time.

01:16:07.100 --> 01:16:08.070
And if you think about it,

01:16:08.070 --> 01:16:10.980
because we're in a 4D
or more world, right?

01:16:10.980 --> 01:16:12.590
So when we talk about
entanglement relations,

01:16:12.590 --> 01:16:16.220
even when we call it the
measuring of special correlations

01:16:16.220 --> 01:16:18.160
in a Bell type experiment,

01:16:18.160 --> 01:16:20.570
we're in fact already
doing causal entanglement

01:16:20.570 --> 01:16:24.800
because those detectors are
at space-time locations.

01:16:24.800 --> 01:16:26.680
And so just getting clear about...

01:16:26.680 --> 01:16:29.280
I started to ask about the
Leggett-Garg inequalities

01:16:29.280 --> 01:16:32.620
and what they really have to
say about temporal correlations

01:16:32.620 --> 01:16:35.450
and how we define macro versus micro

01:16:35.450 --> 01:16:37.960
versus mezzo systems and so on.

01:16:37.960 --> 01:16:39.560
And from special correlations

01:16:39.560 --> 01:16:41.763
and temporal collations came causality.

01:16:42.654 --> 01:16:45.310
And then, also, Priya asked me
to give a talk on causality.

01:16:45.310 --> 01:16:48.160
So... (laughs)

01:16:48.160 --> 01:16:52.240
that's a long-winded answer
to your question, but yeah.

01:16:52.240 --> 01:16:53.935
So I'm new to the causality

01:16:53.935 --> 01:16:55.720
and this is one of the reasons
I'm extraordinarily honored

01:16:55.720 --> 01:16:59.078
and excited to have
Yemima have conversation

01:16:59.078 --> 01:17:02.237
with us tomorrow, because
she is an expert on causation

01:17:02.237 --> 01:17:05.534
and science and in physics, and will bring

01:17:05.534 --> 01:17:09.200
a lot more fruit from what
I've said, I hope. (laughs)

01:17:09.200 --> 01:17:11.500
- Yeah, and I think that...

01:17:11.500 --> 01:17:14.000
Thank you so much, because
I think that thing that

01:17:14.850 --> 01:17:19.840
is confusing to a lot of us
is this idea of determinism,

01:17:19.840 --> 01:17:24.840
causation, transparency of
causes, chains of causation.

01:17:25.150 --> 01:17:28.030
All these things are
really hard to kind of

01:17:28.030 --> 01:17:33.030
clearly parse out when we
are trying to understand.

01:17:33.090 --> 01:17:35.980
You look at a model and
you're trying to figure out

01:17:35.980 --> 01:17:39.700
a chain of causation and
what all you can infer

01:17:39.700 --> 01:17:43.860
from a model, given the
priors that have gone into

01:17:43.860 --> 01:17:46.080
constructing the model
to start with, right?

01:17:46.080 --> 01:17:48.412
So, yeah.

01:17:48.412 --> 01:17:49.740
That was...

01:17:49.740 --> 01:17:52.100
Really looking forward to it.

01:17:52.100 --> 01:17:57.013
Are there any other
questions from the floor?

